OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026", "title": "The Quasi-Riemann Hypothesis (alternate 11/12 proof)", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026/paper2.pdf", "family": "003", "family_title": "The quasi-Riemann hypothesis", "abstract_html": "We establish the quasi-Riemann hypothesis by proving that every Dirichlet <i>L</i>-function, including Riemann's zeta function, has no zeros in the half-plane \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Re}}\\nolimits s\\gt 11/12\\). More generally, we prove the same zero-free half-plane for every finite-order Hecke <i>L</i>-function over \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})\\). In particular, this rules out the existence of Landau–Siegel zeros.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every finite-order Hecke \\(L\\)-function \\(L_K(s,\\nu)\\) has no zeros in \\( \\operatorname{Re}s>\\frac{11}{12}. \\) Consequently, every Dirichlet \\(L\\)-function, including \\(\\zeta(s)\\), has no zeros in this half-plane.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})\\), let \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\), and let \\(\\mathcal O=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(N=N_{K/\\mathbb Q}\\). For \\(a\\in K\\), \\(N(a)=a\\overline a=|a|^2\\); for a nonzero integral ideal \\(\\mathfrak a\\), \\(N(\\mathfrak a)=\\#(\\mathcal O/\\mathfrak a)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu\\) be a finite-order Hecke character of \\(K\\), extended by zero to ideals not coprime to its conductor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every finite-order Hecke \\(L\\)-function \\(L_K(s,\\nu)\\) has no zeros in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;\\frac{11}{12}.\n\\]\nConsequently, every Dirichlet \\(L\\)-function, including \\(\\zeta(s)\\), has no zeros in this half-plane.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a finite-order Hecke character \\(\\nu\\), and let \\(S\\) be as in Proposition 12. Fix \\(0&lt;\\vartheta\\le1/10\\), and put\n\\[\nH=D^{1+\\vartheta},\n\\qquad\nY=H^{1/6}=D^{(1+\\vartheta)/6}.\n\\]\nFor prime ideals \\(p\\notin S\\) with\n\\[\nY/2&lt;N(p)\\le Y,\n\\]\nuse their primary generators. Since\n\\[\n\\chi_n(p^6)=1_{p\\nmid n},\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\n|A_1(D)-A_{p^6}(D)|\n\\le\n\\|W\\|_\\infty\n\\#\\{n:(n,S)=1,\\ p\\mid n,\\ N(n)\\asymp D\\}\n\\ll_W D/Y.\n\\]\nLandau’s prime ideal theorem gives \\(J\\asymp Y/\\log Y\\) such primes. Their sixth powers are distinct rows of norm at most \\(H\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 12 with loss \\(\\varepsilon/2\\), using \\(\\log Y\\ll_\\varepsilon D^{\\varepsilon/2}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|A_1(D)|^2\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac2J\n\\sum_{\\substack{Y/2&lt;N(p)\\le Y\\\\p\\notin S}}\n|A_{p^6}(D)|^2\n+O_W(D^2/Y^2)\\\\\n&amp;\\ll_{\\nu,S,W,\\vartheta,\\varepsilon}\nD^{2+\\vartheta+\\varepsilon}/Y+D^2/Y^2\\\\\n&amp;=\nD^{11/6+5\\vartheta/6+\\varepsilon}\n+\nD^{5/3-\\vartheta/3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(3.2)\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nA_1(D)\\ll_{\\nu,S,W,\\vartheta,\\varepsilon}\nD^{11/12+5\\vartheta/12+\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nGiven a requested exponent loss, first choose \\(\\vartheta&gt;0\\), and then the loss in Proposition 12, sufficiently small. Renaming the resulting loss gives\n\\[\nA_1(D)\\ll_{\\nu,S,W,\\varepsilon}D^{11/12+\\varepsilon}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p><p>Suppose \\(L_K(s,\\nu)\\) has a zero \\(\\varrho\\) with \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\varrho&gt;11/12\\). Choose\n\\[\n0\\ne\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty((1,2)),\\qquad \\phi\\ge0,\n\\qquad W(y)=y^{-\\varrho}\\phi(y).\n\\]\nWith\n\\[\n\\widehat W(s)=\\int_0^\\infty W(y)y^s\\,\\frac{dy}{y},\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\n\\widehat W(\\varrho)=\\int\\phi(y)\\,\\frac{dy}{y}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The function\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_W(s)\n=\n\\int_0^\\infty A_1(D)D^{-s}\\,\\frac{dD}{D}\n\\]\nis holomorphic on \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;11/12\\): (3.3) controls infinity, while compact support makes \\(A_1(D)\\) vanish for sufficiently small \\(D\\). Termwise integration for \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;1\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_W(s)\n=\n\\widehat W(s)\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}\\frac{\\mu(n)\\nu(n)}{N(n)^s}\n=\n\\frac{\\widehat W(s)}{L_K^S(s,\\nu)},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nL_K^S(s,\\nu)\n=\nL_K(s,\\nu)\n\\prod_{p\\in S}(1-\\nu(p)N(p)^{-s}).\n\\]\nHecke’s meromorphic continuation theorem and the identity theorem give\n\\[\nL_K^S(s,\\nu)\\mathcal M_W(s)=\\widehat W(s)\n\\]\non the stated half-plane. The omitted Euler factors are nonzero there. Evaluation at \\(\\varrho\\) yields\n\\[\n0=\\widehat W(\\varrho)&gt;0,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>For the Dirichlet conclusion, let \\(\\chi_{-3}\\) be the nontrivial character modulo \\(3\\). Quadratic base change gives, up to Euler factors nonzero in \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nL_K(s,\\chi\\circ N_{K/\\mathbb Q})\n=\nL(s,\\chi)L(s,\\chi\\chi_{-3}).\n\\]\nThe Hecke conclusion excludes zeros of either factor away from \\(s=1\\). At \\(s=1\\), the only possible pole–zero cancellation is ruled out by Dirichlet’s nonvanishing theorem, which gives \\(L(1,\\chi_{-3})&gt;0\\).</p><p>The introduction also records the following consequences of this fixed zero-free half-plane:</p><p class=\"li\">Rodosskiĭ’s zero-free-region method, in the cited form of Montgomery–Vaughan, bounds the least quadratic nonresidue modulo an odd prime \\(p\\) by a fixed power of \\(\\log p\\), and therefore implies Vinogradov’s bound \\(\\ll_\\varepsilon p^\\varepsilon\\).</p><p class=\"li\">This bound gives deterministic polynomial-time square-root extraction modulo \\(p\\) through Tonelli–Shanks and a deterministic polynomial-time implementation of Miller’s primality test.</p><p class=\"li\">Littlewood’s short Euler-product argument and Dirichlet’s class-number formula give the effective bound\n\\[\nh(D)\\gg\\frac{\\sqrt{|D|}}{\\log\\log|D|}\n\\]\nfor negative fundamental discriminants \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The cited class-group computations, together with Grube’s characterization and reduction for idoneal numbers, give the completeness of Euler’s list of \\(65\\) idoneal numbers when Landau–Siegel zeros are absent.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(x\\ge2\\), \\( \\sup_{\\substack{1\\le q\\le x\\\\(a,q)=1}} \\left| \\pi(x;q,a)-\\frac1{\\varphi(q)}\\int_2^x\\frac{dt}{\\log t} \\right| \\ll x^{11/12}\\log x, \\) with an absolute, effective implied constant.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi(x;q,a)\\) count primes \\(p\\le x\\) satisfying \\(p\\equiv a\\pmod q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varphi\\) be Euler’s totient function.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(x\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{1\\le q\\le x\\\\(a,q)=1}}\n\\left|\n\\pi(x;q,a)-\\frac1{\\varphi(q)}\\int_2^x\\frac{dt}{\\log t}\n\\right|\n\\ll x^{11/12}\\log x,\n\\]\nwith an absolute, effective implied constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the standard explicit-formula argument cited in the paper, Davenport, Chapters 19–20, to the fixed zero-free half-plane in Theorem 1. This gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{1\\le q\\le x\\\\(a,q)=1}}\n\\left|\n\\pi(x;q,a)-\\frac1{\\varphi(q)}\\int_2^x\\frac{dt}{\\log t}\n\\right|\n\\ll x^{11/12}\\log x,\n\\]\nwith an absolute, effective implied constant. The paper provides this deduction by reference rather than a separate expanded proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma B.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(K\\in C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty)^d)\\) supported in \\(I\\) has a representation \\( K(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}b(t)\\prod_{j=1}^d x_j^{it_j}\\,dt, \\qquad x_j>0. \\) For \\(J\\ge0\\) and every even integer \\(q>J+d\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a box \\(I=I_1\\times\\cdots\\times I_d\\), where each \\(I_j\\) is a compact interval in \\((0,\\infty)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth function on a compact set, its \\(C^q\\)-norm is the maximum of the absolute values of all partial derivatives of total order at most \\(q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(K\\in C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty)^d)\\) supported in \\(I\\) has a representation\n\\[\nK(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}b(t)\\prod_{j=1}^d x_j^{it_j}\\,dt,\n\\qquad x_j&gt;0.\n\\tag{B.1}\n\\]\nFor \\(J\\ge0\\) and every even integer \\(q&gt;J+d\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}|b(t)|(1+|t|)^J\\,dt\n\\ll_{I,J,q,d}\\|K\\|_{C^q(I)}.\n\\]</p><p>More specifically, suppose\n\\[\nK_R(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^dW_j(x_j)\\,\nF\\!\\left(R\\prod_{j=1}^d x_j^{a_j}\\right),\n\\qquad R&gt;0,\n\\]\nwhere the real exponents \\(a_j\\) are fixed, \\(W_j\\) is smooth and supported in \\(I_j\\), and\n\\[\n\\|F\\|_{A,q}\n:=\n\\max_{0\\le m\\le q}\\sup_{u&gt;0}\n(1+u)^A|(u\\partial_u)^mF(u)|&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThen the coefficients may be chosen to satisfy\n\\[\n|b_R(t)|\n\\ll\n(1+R)^{-A}(1+|t|)^{-q}\n\\|F\\|_{A,q}\\prod_{j=1}^d\\|W_j\\|_{C^q(I_j)}\n\\tag{B.2}\n\\]\nfor \\(A\\ge0\\) and even \\(q\\ge0\\). In particular, for even \\(q&gt;J+d\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}|b_R(t)|(1+|t|)^J\\,dt\n\\ll\n(1+R)^{-A}\\|F\\|_{A,q}\n\\prod_{j=1}^d\\|W_j\\|_{C^q(I_j)}.\n\\tag{B.3}\n\\]\nThe constants depend only on the displayed fixed parameters, intervals, and exponents.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the Fourier transform in logarithmic coordinates:\n\\[\nb(t)=\\frac1{(2\\pi)^d}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\nK(e^{y_1},\\ldots,e^{y_d})e^{-it\\cdot y}\\,dy.\n\\]\nFourier inversion gives (B.1). Since the intervals \\(I_j\\) are fixed and bounded away from zero, the \\(C^q\\)-norm in logarithmic coordinates is bounded by a constant times \\(\\|K\\|_{C^q(I)}\\).</p><p>Applying \\((1-\\Delta_y)^{q/2}\\) under the integral gives\n\\[\n|b(t)|\\ll_{I,q,d}(1+|t|)^{-q}\\|K\\|_{C^q(I)}\n\\]\nfor even \\(q\\ge0\\). Multiplication by \\((1+|t|)^J\\) and integration proves the assertion when \\(q&gt;J+d\\).</p><p>For the specified \\(K_R\\), the argument of \\(F\\) is comparable to \\(R\\) on the fixed box \\(I\\). The chain rule and the same pointwise coefficient estimate give (B.2), and integration gives (B.3). Thus separation preserves the decay in \\(R\\), with cost controlled by finitely many derivatives of the original weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma B.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(|c_r|\\le w_r\\) and smooth kernels \\(K_r\\) supported in \\(I^2\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix compact intervals \\(I\\subset\\operatorname{int}I_*\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) and an integer \\(m\\ge0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j=1,2\\), let\n\\[\nS_{j,r}(U)=\\sum_n a_{j,r}(n)U(x_{j,r,n}),\n\\qquad x_{j,r,n}&gt;0,\n\\]\nbe finite sums indexed by a finite set of rows \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_r\\ge0\\), and suppose that for every \\(U\\in C_c^\\infty(I_*)\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_r w_r|S_{j,r}(U)|^2\n\\le M_j\\|U\\|_{C^m(I_*)}^2,\n\\qquad j=1,2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(|c_r|\\le w_r\\) and smooth kernels \\(K_r\\) supported in \\(I^2\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sum_r c_r\\sum_{n_1,n_2}\na_{1,r}(n_1)\\overline{a_{2,r}(n_2)}\nK_r(x_{1,r,n_1},x_{2,r,n_2})\n\\right|\n\\ll_{I,I_*,m}\n\\sqrt{M_1M_2}\\,\n\\sup_r\\|K_r\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I^2)}.\n\\tag{B.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a real \\(V\\in C_c^\\infty(I_*)\\) equal to one on \\(I\\), and put\n\\[\nU_t(x)=V(x)x^{it}.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 1 to each \\(K_r\\), with \\(d=2\\). Replacing the second Mellin variable by its negative and multiplying by \\(V(x)V(y)\\) gives\n\\[\nK_r(x,y)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\nb_r(s,t)U_s(x)\\overline{U_t(y)}\\,ds\\,dt,\n\\]\nwhere, for even \\(q\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_r|b_r(s,t)|\n\\ll_{I,q}\n\\frac{\\sup_r\\|K_r\\|_{C^q(I^2)}}\n{(1+|s|+|t|)^q}.\n\\]</p><p>The hypothesis applies to \\(U_t\\), and\n\\[\n\\|U_t\\|_{C^m(I_*)}\\ll_{I,I_*,m}(1+|t|)^m.\n\\]\nTake the common bound for \\(b_r\\) before integrating, and apply Cauchy–Schwarz in \\(r\\). The absolute value in (B.4) is at most a constant times\n\\[\n\\sqrt{M_1M_2}\\sup_r\\|K_r\\|_{C^q(I^2)}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\n\\frac{(1+|s|)^m(1+|t|)^m}\n{(1+|s|+|t|)^q}\\,ds\\,dt.\n\\tag{B.5}\n\\]\nTaking \\(q=2m+4\\) makes the integral converge.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma B.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Whenever the right-hand side is finite, \\( \\sum_r w_r \\left| \\sum_\\iota\\int_{\\mathbb R^d} c_{\\iota,r}(t)F_{\\iota,t}(r)\\,dt \\right|^2 \\le M\\sum_\\iota\\int_{\\mathbb R^d} b_\\iota(t)\\sum_r w_r|F_{\\iota,t}(r)|^2\\,dt. \\) The …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r,\\iota\\) range over finite sets, let \\(d\\ge1\\), and let \\(w_r\\ge0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose measurable coefficients \\(c_{\\iota,r}\\) satisfy\n\\[\n|c_{\\iota,r}(t)|\\le b_\\iota(t),\n\\qquad\nM:=\\sum_\\iota\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}b_\\iota(t)\\,dt&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nwhere \\(b_\\iota:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_{\\iota,t}(r)\\) be measurable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Whenever the right-hand side is finite,\n\\[\n\\sum_r w_r\n\\left|\n\\sum_\\iota\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\nc_{\\iota,r}(t)F_{\\iota,t}(r)\\,dt\n\\right|^2\n\\le\nM\\sum_\\iota\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\nb_\\iota(t)\\sum_r w_r|F_{\\iota,t}(r)|^2\\,dt.\n\\tag{B.6}\n\\]\nThe same assertion holds for finite sums with the integrals omitted.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each fixed \\(r\\), weighted Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sum_\\iota\\int c_{\\iota,r}(t)F_{\\iota,t}(r)\\,dt\n\\right|^2\n\\le\nM\\sum_\\iota\\int b_\\iota(t)|F_{\\iota,t}(r)|^2\\,dt.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(w_r\\) and sum over \\(r\\). The same argument with sums in place of integrals proves the finite version.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On the fixed ray class group there are a unit-modulus function \\(G\\) and a symmetric \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\)-valued bicharacter \\(\\mathcal R\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})\\), \\(\\mathcal O=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\), and \\(N=N_{K/\\mathbb Q}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite set \\(S\\) of prime ideals containing the primes above \\(2,3\\) and those dividing the conductor of the fixed finite-order Hecke character \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An element is primary if it is congruent to \\(1\\pmod3\\). Every ideal prime to \\(3\\) is represented by its unique primary generator.</p><p class=\"li\">Ideal divisor sums use primary generators and count each ideal divisor once. Write \\(\\mu\\) for the ideal Möbius function and \\(\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}\\) for the ideal divisor function.</p><p class=\"li\">For primary \\(n\\) prime to \\(6\\), put\n\\[\n\\chi_n(u)=(u/n)_6,\n\\]\nthe sextic residue symbol, extended by zero when \\((u,n)\\ne1\\). For the unit ideal, \\(\\chi_1(u)=1\\), including at \\(u=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\ne(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{4\\pi i\\,\\operatorname{Im}z}{\\sqrt3}\\right),\n\\qquad\n\\alpha(n)=\\frac n{|n|}.\n\\]\nFor squarefree primary \\(n\\) prime to \\(S\\), define\n\\[\n\\gamma_j(n)=N(n)^{-1/2}\n\\sum_{x\\bmod n}\\chi_n(x)^j e(x/n).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a fixed ray class group, of modulus supported on \\(S\\), through which \\(\\nu\\) and all fixed reciprocity factors below factor. Let \\(\\xi\\) range over its characters, and set\n\\[\na_\\xi(n)=\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\,\\gamma_2(n)\\xi(n)\n\\]\nfor squarefree primary \\(n\\) prime to \\(S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On the fixed ray class group there are a unit-modulus function \\(G\\) and a symmetric \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\)-valued bicharacter \\(\\mathcal R\\). For primary \\(a,b,n\\) prime to \\(S\\), with \\((a,b)=1\\) and \\(n\\) squarefree,\n\\[\n\\chi_b(a)=\\mathcal R(a,b)\\chi_a(b),\n\\qquad\nG(ab)=G(a)G(b)\\mathcal R(a,b),\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\gamma_2(n)^3=\\mu(n)\\alpha(n),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_1(n)\\gamma_2(n)=\\mu(n)\\alpha(n)G(n),\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\\[\nG(n)=\\overline{\\chi_n(4)}\\,\\gamma_3(n),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_1(n)\\gamma_{-1}(n)=\\chi_n(-1).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\,\\gamma_2(n)\\gamma_1(n)=\\mu(n)G(n),\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mu(n)\\gamma_{-1}(n)\n=\n\\chi_n(-1)G(n)^{-1}\n\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\,\\gamma_2(n),\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\\[\na_\\xi(ab)=a_\\xi(a)a_\\xi(b)\\chi_b(a)^4\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nwhen \\(a,b\\) are also squarefree, and\n\\[\n\\chi_a(-1)\\overline{G(a)}G(b)\\mathcal R(a,b)\n=\nG(ba^{-1}).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nThe multiplicative identity for \\(G\\) holds on all classes of the fixed ray class group; the quotient in (4.7) is taken in that group.</p>", "proof": "<p>We give the arithmetic calculation from Appendix A.1.</p><p>For multiplicative characters \\(A,B\\) of \\((\\mathcal O/(p))^\\times\\), extended by zero at zero, write\n\\[\nJ(A,B)=\\sum_{x\\bmod p}A(x)B(1-x).\n\\]\nFix a primary prime \\(p\\notin S\\). The substitution \\(u=2x-1\\) gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{x\\bmod p:4x(1-x)=y\\}\n=\n1+\\chi_p^3(1-y).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\chi_p(4)J(\\chi_p,\\chi_p)=J(\\chi_p,\\chi_p^3).\n\\]\nThe Gauss–Jacobi identity yields\n\\[\n\\frac{\\gamma_1(p)\\gamma_3(p)}{\\gamma_4(p)}\n=\n\\frac{J(\\chi_p,\\chi_p^3)}{\\sqrt{N(p)}}\n=\n\\chi_p(4)\\frac{\\gamma_1(p)^2}{\\gamma_2(p)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(j\\not\\equiv0\\pmod6\\),\n\\[\n\\gamma_j(p)\\gamma_{-j}(p)=\\chi_p(-1)^j.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\gamma_2(p)\\gamma_4(p)=1\\). Combining these identities and cancelling \\(\\gamma_1(p)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\gamma_1(p)\\gamma_2(p)\n=\n\\overline{\\chi_p(4)}\\,\\gamma_3(p)\\gamma_2(p)^3.\n\\]</p><p>To evaluate \\(\\gamma_2(p)^3\\), put \\(q=N(p)\\). The Jacobi sum \\(J(\\chi_p^2,\\chi_p^2)\\) lies in \\(\\mathcal O\\) and has norm \\(q\\). For \\(0\\le\\ell\\le(q-1)/3\\), the exponent \\((q-1)/3+\\ell\\) lies strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(q-1\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\bmod p}x^{(q-1)/3+\\ell}=0\n\\]\nin \\(\\mathcal O/(p)\\). Expanding the binomial therefore gives\n\\[\nJ(\\chi_p^2,\\chi_p^2)\n\\equiv\n\\sum_{x\\bmod p}x^{(q-1)/3}(1-x)^{(q-1)/3}\n\\equiv0\\pmod p.\n\\]\nThus \\(J(\\chi_p^2,\\chi_p^2)/p\\) is a unit.</p><p>For \\(x\\notin\\{0,1\\}\\), write\n\\[\n\\chi_p^2(x(1-x))=\\omega^{j_x},\n\\qquad j_x\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\prod_{x\\ne0,1}x(1-x)=1,\n\\]\nwe have \\(\\sum_{x\\ne0,1}j_x\\equiv0\\pmod3\\). As \\((\\omega-1)^2\\) generates \\((3)\\),\n\\[\nJ(\\chi_p^2,\\chi_p^2)\n\\equiv(q-2)+(\\omega-1)\\sum_{x\\ne0,1}j_x\n\\equiv-1\\pmod3.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(p\\equiv1\\pmod3\\), this fixes the unit:\n\\[\nJ(\\chi_p^2,\\chi_p^2)=-p.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\gamma_2(p)^3\n=\n\\frac{\\gamma_2(p)^2}{\\gamma_4(p)}\n=\n\\frac{J(\\chi_p^2,\\chi_p^2)}{\\sqrt{N(p)}}\n=\n-\\alpha(p).\n\\]\nThe Chinese remainder theorem extends the prime identities to (4.2) for squarefree \\(n\\), with one minus sign for each prime factor accounting for \\(\\mu(n)\\).</p><p>For coprime squarefree primary \\(a,b\\),\n\\[\n\\gamma_2(ab)\n=\n\\gamma_2(a)\\gamma_2(b)\\chi_a(b)^2\\chi_b(a)^2.\n\\]\nCubic reciprocity gives \\(\\chi_a(b)^2=\\chi_b(a)^2\\). Multiplication by \\(\\overline{\\alpha(ab)}\\xi(ab)\\) proves (4.6).</p><p>We next evaluate the quadratic Gauss sum. For \\(c\\in\\mathcal O\\) prime to \\(2\\),\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(c)\n:=\n|c|^{-1}\\sum_{x\\bmod c}e(x^2/c)\n=\n\\frac12\\sum_{y\\bmod2\\mathcal O}e(-cy^2/4).\n\\tag{A.1}\n\\]\nTo justify this, apply Poisson summation to\n\\[\ne(z^2/c)e^{-\\pi\\eta|z|^2},\n\\qquad \\eta&gt;0.\n\\]\nIts Fourier transform is\n\\[\n\\frac{|c|}{2\\sqrt{r_\\eta}}\ne^{-\\pi\\eta N(c)|y|^2/(4r_\\eta)}\ne(-cy^2/(4r_\\eta)),\n\\qquad\nr_\\eta=1+\\frac{3N(c)\\eta^2}{16}.\n\\]\nDivide by the Gaussian mass\n\\[\n\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal O}e^{-\\pi\\eta|z|^2}\\asymp\\eta^{-1}.\n\\]\nOn the Fourier side, replacing \\(r_\\eta\\) by \\(1\\) has total error\n\\[\nO_c\\!\\left(\\eta^2\\sum_{y\\in\\mathcal O}|y|^2e^{-C_c\\eta|y|^2}\\right)\n=O_c(1).\n\\]\nThe normalized error tends to zero. Grouping the original sum modulo \\(c\\), and the Fourier sum modulo \\(2\\mathcal O\\), then letting \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\), proves (A.1).</p><p>For \\(c=a+b\\omega\\),\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(a+b\\omega)\n=\n\\frac{1+i^{-b}+i^a+i^{b-a}}2.\n\\]\nIt depends only on \\(c\\bmod4\\mathcal O\\) and is invariant under multiplication by a square in \\((\\mathcal O/4\\mathcal O)^\\times\\). Representatives for the four square classes and their values are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\nc&amp;1&amp;-1&amp;\\lambda&amp;-\\lambda\\\\ \\hline\n\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(c)&amp;1&amp;1&amp;i&amp;-i\n\\end{array},\n\\qquad \\lambda=1+2\\omega.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mathcal R(c_1,c_2)\n=\n\\frac{\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(c_1c_2)}\n{\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(c_1)\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(c_2)}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal R((-1)^e\\lambda^f,(-1)^g\\lambda^h)\n=\n(-1)^{eh+fg+fh},\n\\qquad e,f,g,h\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\]\nand is a symmetric bicharacter.</p><p>For a prime \\(p\\), grouping by \\(y=x^2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(p)\n=\n|p|^{-1}\\sum_{y\\bmod p}(1+\\chi_p^3(y))e(y/p)\n=\n\\gamma_3(p).\n\\]\nThe Chinese remainder theorem extends this equality to squarefree \\(n\\). Together with cubic reciprocity, it identifies \\(\\mathcal R(a,b)\\) with \\(\\chi_b(a)/\\chi_a(b)\\). Furthermore,\n\\[\n\\overline{\\chi_c(4)}\n=\n(-2/c)_3\n=\n(c/(-2))_3\n\\]\nis a character modulo \\(2\\). Hence\n\\[\nG(c)=\\overline{\\chi_c(4)}\\,\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{quad}}(c)\n\\]\nfactors through a fixed ray class group and satisfies (4.1), with\n\\[\n\\mathcal R(c,c)=\\chi_c(-1).\n\\]</p><p>The multiplicative identity gives\n\\[\nG(a^{-1})=\\chi_a(-1)\\overline{G(a)}.\n\\]\nSymmetry and multiplicativity of \\(\\mathcal R\\) then give (4.7).</p><p>Finally, the inverse-character Gauss identity gives the second identity in (4.3). Multiplying the second identity in (4.2) by \\(\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\) proves (4.4), and combining it with the inverse-character identity proves (4.5).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "With \\(\\operatorname{rad}\\mathfrak r\\) the product of the distinct prime divisors of \\(\\mathfrak r\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over \\(\\mathcal O=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\), with \\(N\\) and \\(e\\) as in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H&gt;0\\), let \\(\\mathfrak r\\) be a nonzero ideal, and let \\(\\Phi(N(k)/\\mathcal H)\\) be a smooth radial Schwartz weight on the row lattice.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\chi\\) be a primitive multiplicative character modulo \\(\\mathfrak m\\), extended by zero on nonunits.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose fixed generators of ideals, primary when prime to \\(3\\), and put\n\\[\n\\gamma(\\chi)=N(\\mathfrak m)^{-1/2}\n\\sum_{x\\bmod\\mathfrak m}\\chi(x)e(x/\\mathfrak m).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\widehat\\Phi(|w|^2)\n=\n\\frac2{\\sqrt3}\\int_{\\mathbb C}\\Phi(|z|^2)e(-zw)\\,dx\\,dy,\n\\qquad z=x+iy.\n\\]\nThe measure is normalized so that \\(\\mathcal O\\) has covolume one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With \\(\\operatorname{rad}\\mathfrak r\\) the product of the distinct prime divisors of \\(\\mathfrak r\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal O}\n\\chi(k)1_{(k,\\mathfrak r)=1}\\Phi(N(k)/\\mathcal H)\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal H\\gamma(\\chi)}{\\sqrt{N(\\mathfrak m)}}\n\\sum_{d\\mid\\operatorname{rad}\\mathfrak r}\n\\frac{\\mu(d)\\chi(d)}{N(d)}\n\\sum_{h\\in\\mathcal O}\n\\overline{\\chi(h)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{\\mathcal HN(h)}{N(d)N(\\mathfrak m)}\n\\right).\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nFor nonprincipal \\(\\chi\\), the zero frequency vanishes. For the principal primitive character, whose modulus is \\(1\\), the zero-frequency contribution is\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\widehat\\Phi(0)\n\\prod_{p\\mid\\mathfrak r}\\left(1-\\frac1{N(p)}\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Inclusion–exclusion followed by \\(k=d\\ell\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_k\\chi(k)1_{(k,\\mathfrak r)=1}\\Phi(N(k)/\\mathcal H)\n=\n\\sum_{d\\mid\\operatorname{rad}\\mathfrak r}\n\\mu(d)\\chi(d)\n\\sum_\\ell\\chi(\\ell)\n\\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{N(\\ell)}{\\mathcal H/N(d)}\\right).\n\\]\nApply lattice Poisson summation in \\(\\ell\\), on residue classes modulo \\(\\mathfrak m\\), at scale \\(\\mathcal H/N(d)\\). The primitive Gauss-sum identity evaluates the finite Fourier transform as\n\\[\n\\sqrt{N(\\mathfrak m)}\\,\\gamma(\\chi)\\overline{\\chi(h)}\n\\]\nfor every \\(h\\), giving (4.8).</p><p>If \\(\\chi\\) is nonprincipal, \\(\\overline{\\chi(0)}=0\\). For the principal character, summing \\(\\mu(d)/N(d)\\) over \\(d\\mid\\operatorname{rad}\\mathfrak r\\) gives the stated zero-frequency term.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 4.4; the family below is Definition 4.3, equation (4.9)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal E_{\\mathfrak r}(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W) \\le \\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(\\mathfrak r_0) \\sum_{d\\mid\\mathfrak r_0} \\mathcal E\\!\\left( \\mathcal H,\\frac X{N(d)},FN(d);\\xi,W \\right). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the field, excluded prime set \\(S\\), fixed ray class characters \\(\\xi\\), and coefficients\n\\[\na_\\xi(n)=\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\gamma_2(n)\\xi(n)\n\\]\nfrom Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">A star restricts an ideal index to squarefree ideals prime to \\(S\\), represented by their primary generators.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\mathcal H,X&gt;0\\), \\(F\\ge1\\), and \\(W\\in C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W)\n=\n\\frac1{XF}\n\\sum_{\\substack{F\\le N(f)&lt;2F\\\\(f,S)=1}}^{*}\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(k)\\le\\mathcal H}\n\\left|\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4W(N(n)/X)\n\\right|^2.\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal E_{\\mathfrak r}\\) denote the same expression with the extra column restriction \\((n,\\mathfrak r)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathfrak r_0\\) be the product of the primes dividing \\(\\mathfrak r\\) outside \\(S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathfrak r}(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W)\n\\le\n\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(\\mathfrak r_0)\n\\sum_{d\\mid\\mathfrak r_0}\n\\mathcal E\\!\\left(\n\\mathcal H,\\frac X{N(d)},FN(d);\\xi,W\n\\right).\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(C_{\\mathfrak r}(X;k,f)\\) denote the inner column sum, with \\(\\xi,W\\) fixed. Inclusion–exclusion, \\(n=dm\\), and (4.6) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\mathfrak r}(X;k,f)\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{d\\mid\\mathfrak r_0}\\mu(d)\n\\sum_{\\substack{(n,S)=1\\\\d\\mid n}}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4W(N(n)/X)\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{\\substack{d\\mid\\mathfrak r_0\\\\(d,f)=1}}\n\\mu(d)a_\\xi(d)\\chi_d(k)\\chi_d(f)^4\nC_1(X/N(d);k,df).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(\\chi_m(d)^4\\) enforces \\((m,d)=1\\) and combines with \\(\\chi_m(f)^4\\); terms with \\((d,f)\\ne1\\) vanish.</p><p>Each exterior coefficient has modulus at most one. Apply Cauchy–Schwarz in \\(d\\), then enlarge the injective image \\(f\\mapsto df\\) to the squarefree range\n\\[\nFN(d)\\le N(df)&lt;2FN(d).\n\\]\nThe normalizing product remains unchanged:\n\\[\n(X/N(d))(FN(d))=XF.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 6.2 and Lemma 6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The quantity \\(T(X;\\Psi)\\) is a sum of \\(O_{\\Psi_0,S}(2^{|\\mathcal P|})\\) terms …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over \\(\\mathcal O=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\), with \\(S,N,e,\\alpha,\\gamma_j\\) as in Proposition 4, and put \\(\\lambda=1+2\\omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Psi\\) be completely multiplicative on nonzero integral ideals prime to \\(3\\), with zero extension at every prime in \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(W\\in C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), put \\(V_*(y)=\\sqrt y\\,W(y)\\), and define\n\\[\nT(X;\\Psi)\n=\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\n\\sum_{\\substack{b\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\(b,S)=1}}\n\\frac{\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\gamma_2(n)\\Psi(n)\n\\overline{\\alpha(b)}^{\\,3}\\Psi(b)^3}\n{\\sqrt{N(n)}\\,N(b)}\nV_*(N(n)N(b)^3/X).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\theta\\) be Kubota’s cubic theta function in the normalization\n\\[\n\\theta(z,v)\n=\n\\frac{3^{5/2}}2v^{2/3}\n+\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\ell\\in\\lambda^{-3}\\mathcal O\\\\\\ell\\ne0}}\n\\tau(\\ell)vK_{1/3}(4\\pi|\\ell|v)\n\\exp(2\\pi i(\\ell z+\\overline{\\ell z})).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\gamma_0=I,\\qquad\n\\gamma_+=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\\\omega&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_-=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\\\omega^2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nDefine \\(d_\\sigma\\), \\(\\sigma\\in\\{0,+,-\\}\\), by\n\\[\n\\overline{\\theta(\\gamma_\\sigma(z,v))}\n=\n\\frac{3^{5/2}}2\\,1_{\\sigma=0}v^{2/3}\n+\n\\sum_{0\\ne\\ell\\in\\lambda^{-4}\\mathcal O}\nd_\\sigma(\\ell)vK_{1/3}(4\\pi|\\ell|v)\n\\exp(2\\pi i(\\ell z+\\overline{\\ell z})).\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nThus \\(d_0(\\ell)=\\overline{\\tau(-\\ell)}\\), with \\(\\tau\\) zero outside \\(\\lambda^{-3}\\mathcal O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a primary prime \\(p\\notin S\\), every power of \\(\\chi_p\\), including its zeroth power, is extended by zero on multiples of \\(p\\). Define\n\\[\nB_{p,j}(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\chi_p(x)^{-j-2},&amp;j\\ne0,4,\\\\\nN(p)^{-1/2}(-1+N(p)1_{p\\mid x}),&amp;j=4,\\\\\nN(p)^{-1/2}\\chi_p(x)^{-2},&amp;j=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(B_{p,1}(x)=\\chi_p(x)^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\widehat V_*(s)=\\int_0^\\infty V_*(x)x^s\\,\\frac{dx}{x},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nV_*^\\sharp(x)\n=\n\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_{(0)}\n\\widehat V_*(-t)\n\\frac{\\Gamma(7/6+t)\\Gamma(5/6+t)}\n{\\Gamma(7/6-t)\\Gamma(5/6-t)}\n\\left(\\frac{(2\\pi)^4x}{27}\\right)^{-t}\\,dt.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a ray class character \\(\\Psi_0\\) of conductor supported on \\(S\\), extended by zero at \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be a finite set of primary primes outside \\(S\\), choose \\(j_p\\in\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}\\), and put\n\\[\n\\Psi(n)=\\Psi_0(n)\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\chi_p(n)^{j_p}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The quantity \\(T(X;\\Psi)\\) is a sum of \\(O_{\\Psi_0,S}(2^{|\\mathcal P|})\\) terms\n\\[\nC\\sum_{0\\ne\\ell\\in\\lambda^{-4}\\mathcal O}\n\\frac{d(\\ell)\\alpha(\\ell)}{\\sqrt{N(\\ell)}}\n\\psi(\\lambda^4\\ell)\n\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal A}B_{p,j_p}(\\lambda^4\\ell)\nV_*^\\sharp\\!\\left(\\frac{N(\\ell)X}{N(c)^2}\\right),\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\{p\\in\\mathcal P:j_p\\ne0\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\mathcal P,\n\\qquad\nc=c_0\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal A}p.\n\\]\nHere \\(|C|\\ll_{\\Psi_0,S}1\\), and \\((d,\\psi,c_0)\\) belongs to a fixed finite family depending only on \\(\\Psi_0,S\\), with\n\\[\nd\\in\\{d_0,d_+,d_-\\},\\qquad c_0\\ne0,\n\\]\nand \\(\\psi\\) a unit-modulus additive character on \\(\\mathcal O\\).</p><p>The transformation also has the following uniformity, originally Lemma 6.3. Fix \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{fix}}\\) and its exponents, and let\n\\[\n\\Psi_{k_0}(n)\n=\n\\Psi_0(n)\n\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{fix}}}\\chi_p(n)^{j_p}\n\\prod_{p\\mid k_0}\\chi_p(n),\n\\]\nwhere \\(k_0\\) is squarefree, primary, and prime to \\(S\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{fix}}}p\\). The summands may be indexed by \\((h,\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{fix}})\\), where \\(h\\) ranges over a fixed finite set depending only on \\(\\Psi_0,S\\), and\n\\[\n\\{p\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{fix}}:j_p\\ne0\\}\n\\subseteq\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{fix}}\n\\subseteq\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{fix}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{fix}}\\cup\\{p:p\\mid k_0\\}.\n\\]\nFor each fixed index, \\((d,\\psi,c_0)\\) depends on \\(k_0\\) only through its ray class modulo a fixed ideal supported on \\(S\\). Zero scalar coefficients are permitted.</p><p>The cusp-coefficient support and weight estimates in Lemma 7.2 are part of this transformation package.</p>", "proof": "<p>We follow Appendix A.2.</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\phi(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\chi_n(\\lambda)^2\\Psi_0(n),&amp;\nn\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\ (n,S)=1,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Theta_\\Psi(z,v)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\ell\\in\\lambda^{-3}\\mathcal O\\\\\\ell\\ne0}}\n\\overline{\\tau(-\\ell)}\\phi(\\lambda^3\\ell)\n\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\chi_p(\\lambda^3\\ell)^{j_p}\nvK_{1/3}(4\\pi|\\ell|v)\\check e(\\ell z).\n\\tag{A.2}\n\\]\nThe realization in Lemma 7.1 applies with \\(\\Psi\\) in place of \\(\\Psi_k\\).</p><p>Choose a fixed nonzero \\(L\\), supported on \\(S\\), divisible by the conductor of \\(\\Psi_0\\), every prime in \\(S\\), and sufficiently high powers of the primes above \\(2,3\\). The supplementary cubic reciprocity law makes \\(\\phi\\) periodic modulo \\(L\\). Write\n\\[\n\\widehat\\phi(h_0)=\n\\frac1{N(L)}\\sum_{x\\bmod L}\\phi(x)e(-h_0x/L),\n\\qquad |\\widehat\\phi(h_0)|\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\), define\n\\[\nC_{p,j}(h_p)\n=\n\\frac1{N(p)}\\sum_{y\\bmod p}\\chi_p(y)^j e(-h_py/p).\n\\]\nFinite Fourier inversion gives\n\\[\n\\chi_p(x)^j\n=\n\\sum_{h_p\\bmod p}C_{p,j}(h_p)e(h_px/p).\n\\tag{A.3}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\Theta_\\Psi(z,v)\n=\n\\sum_{\\mathbf h}c_F(\\mathbf h)\n\\overline{\\theta(z+z_{\\mathbf h},v)},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nz_{\\mathbf h}\n=\n\\lambda^2\\left(\\frac{h_0}{L}\n+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\frac{h_p}{p}\\right),\n\\qquad\nc_F(\\mathbf h)\n=\n\\widehat\\phi(h_0)\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P}C_{p,j_p}(h_p).\n\\tag{A.4}\n\\]\nThe constant terms cancel because\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathbf h}c_F(\\mathbf h)\n=\n\\phi(0)\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\chi_p(0)^{j_p}=0.\n\\]</p><p>Direct evaluation gives\n\\[\nC_{p,j}(h_p)=\n\\begin{cases}\nN(p)^{-1/2}\\chi_p(-1)^j\\gamma_j(p)\\chi_p(h_p)^{-j},\n&amp;j\\ne0,\\ h_p\\ne0,\\\\\n0,&amp;j\\ne0,\\ h_p=0,\\\\\n-N(p)^{-1},&amp;j=0,\\ h_p\\ne0,\\\\\n1-N(p)^{-1},&amp;j=0,\\ h_p=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{A.5}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal A(\\mathbf h)=\\{p:h_p\\ne0\\}\\). Nonzero coefficients require every prime with \\(j_p\\ne0\\) to be active. Put\n\\[\nr=\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal A(\\mathbf h)}p.\n\\]\nIf \\(c_0\\) is the reduced denominator of \\(\\lambda^2h_0/L\\), then the reduced denominator of \\(z_{\\mathbf h}\\) is \\(c=c_0r\\), up to a unit: an active prime cannot cancel, and at primes dividing \\(L\\) the other summands are integral. There are \\(O_L(2^{|\\mathcal P|})\\) groups indexed by \\(h_0\\) and the active set.</p><p>To determine the cusp expansion, put \\(M=\\lambda^{12}L^4\\). Choose representatives \\(h_p\\equiv0\\pmod{M^2}\\), and restrict \\(r\\) to a residue class modulo \\(M^2\\). Write \\(z_{\\mathbf h}=a/c\\) in lowest terms, normalizing \\(a\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) if \\(\\lambda\\mid c\\), and \\(c\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) otherwise. The fixed choices determine \\(a\\bmod Mc_0\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\delta'\\) by the Chinese remainder theorem so that\n\\[\na\\delta'\\equiv1\\pmod{q^{v_q(Mc_0)}}\\quad(q\\mid c_0),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta'\\equiv0\\pmod{q^{v_q(M)}}\\quad(q\\mid M,\\ q\\nmid c_0),\n\\qquad\na\\delta'\\equiv1\\pmod r.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nb_g=\\frac{a\\delta'-1}{c},\n\\qquad\ng=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b_g\\\\c&amp;\\delta'\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nb_g\\equiv\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&amp;q\\mid c_0,\\\\\n-c^{-1},&amp;q\\mid M,\\ q\\nmid c_0,\n\\end{cases}\n\\pmod{q^{v_q(M)}}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nH=\n\\begin{cases}\nI,&amp;3\\mid c,\\\\{}[2mm]\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\u_0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n&amp;v_\\lambda(c)=1,\\quad\nu_0\\in\\{\\lambda,-\\lambda\\},\\ u_0\\equiv c\\pmod3,\\\\{}[3mm]\n\\begin{pmatrix}u_0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n&amp;(c,\\lambda)=1,\\quad u_0\\equiv a\\pmod3,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nusing a fixed set of representatives for \\(u_0\\) in the last case. Then \\(g_1=gH^{-1}\\equiv I\\pmod3\\).</p><p>The invariances\n\\[\n\\theta(\\gamma w)=\\theta(w)\\quad(\\gamma\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)),\n\\qquad\n\\theta(z+t,v)=\\theta(z,v)\\quad(t\\in\\mathbb Z+3\\mathcal O)\n\\]\nreduce \\(\\overline{\\theta(Hw)}\\) to one of the three cusp expansions in (6.8), whose coefficient bounds are given in Lemma 7.2.</p><p>Kubota’s automorphy law is\n\\[\n\\theta(g_1w)=\\kappa(g_1)\\theta(w),\n\\qquad\n\\kappa(g_1)=(c_1/a_1)_3\n\\]\nfor \\(g_1=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}a_1&amp;b_1\\\\c_1&amp;d_1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\equiv I\\pmod3\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\overline{\\theta(z+a/c,v)}\n=\n\\overline{\\kappa(g_1)}\n\\overline{\\theta(Hg^{-1}(z+a/c,v))}.\n\\tag{A.8}\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\ng^{-1}(z+a/c,v)\n=\n\\left(\n-\\frac{\\delta'}c-\\frac{\\overline z}{c^2(v^2+|z|^2)},\n\\frac{v}{N(c)(v^2+|z|^2)}\n\\right).\n\\tag{A.9}\n\\]\nAt \\(z=0\\), the Fourier phase is \\(\\check e(-\\delta'\\ell/c)\\).</p><p>The determinant equation and cubic reciprocity give\n\\[\n\\kappa(g_1)=\n\\begin{cases}\n(c_0/a)_3(a/r)_3,&amp;3\\mid c,\\\\\n(-u_0/(a-u_0b_g))_3((c_0/u_0)/a)_3(a/r)_3,\n&amp;v_\\lambda(c)=1,\\\\\n(a/c_0)_3(a/r)_3,&amp;(c,\\lambda)=1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{A.10}\n\\]\nFor the middle case, the required congruences are\n\\[\na(c-u_0\\delta')\\equiv-u_0\\pmod{a-u_0b_g},\n\\qquad\nb_gc\\equiv-1\\pmod a.\n\\]\nFor \\((c,\\lambda)=1\\),\n\\[\n-b_gc=1-a\\delta'\\equiv1\\pmod9\n\\]\nremoves the supplementary factors, and reciprocity at the remaining primes gives\n\\[\n(\\delta'/(-b_gc))_3=1.\n\\]\nAll factors other than \\((a/r)_3\\) depend only on fixed residues at \\(S\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\kappa(g_1)=\\kappa_0\\prod_{p\\mid r}\\chi_p(a)^2,\n\\qquad |\\kappa_0|=1.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(D_0=\\lambda^3c_0\\), and for each active \\(p\\) define modulo \\(p\\)\n\\[\n\\sigma_p=\\lambda^2c/p,\n\\qquad\n\\epsilon_p=-((\\lambda^3c/p)\\sigma_p)^{-1}.\n\\]\nThen \\(a\\equiv\\sigma_ph_p\\pmod p\\), and additive Chinese remainder decomposition gives\n\\[\n\\check e(-\\delta'\\ell/c)\n=\n\\psi(\\lambda^4\\ell)\n\\prod_{p\\mid r}\ne(\\epsilon_ph_p^{-1}\\lambda^4\\ell/p),\n\\tag{A.11}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\psi(\\lambda^4\\ell)\n=\ne(-\\delta_0'r^{-1}\\lambda^4\\ell/D_0),\n\\qquad\n\\delta_0'=\\delta'\\bmod D_0.\n\\]\nThe fixed choices make \\(\\psi\\) range over a fixed finite family.</p><p>The local transformation is\n\\[\n\\sum_{h_p\\ne0}\nC_{p,j}(h_p)\\chi_p(a)^{-2}\ne(\\epsilon_ph_p^{-1}\\lambda^4\\ell/p)\n=\n\\chi_p(\\sigma_p)^{-2}\\omega_{p,j}\nB_{p,j}(\\lambda^4\\ell),\n\\tag{A.12}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\omega_{p,j}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\chi_p(-1)^j\\gamma_j(p)\\gamma_{j+2}(p)\n\\chi_p(\\epsilon_p)^{-j-2},&amp;j\\ne0,4,\\\\\n\\gamma_4(p),&amp;j=4,\\\\\n-\\gamma_2(p)\\chi_p(\\epsilon_p)^{-2},&amp;j=0\\text{ active}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nEach \\(\\omega_{p,j}\\) has modulus one.</p><p>Indeed, setting \\(y=h_p^{-1}\\) changes the character exponent to \\(j+2\\):\n\\[\n\\chi_p(h_p)^{-j}\\chi_p(a)^{-2}\n=\n\\chi_p(\\sigma_p)^{-2}\\chi_p(y)^{j+2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(j\\ne0,4\\), a nontrivial Gauss sum gives \\(B_{p,j}\\). For \\(j=4\\), the sum is\n\\[\n\\sum_{y\\ne0}e(\\lambda^4\\ell y/p)\n=\n-1+N(p)1_{p\\mid\\lambda^4\\ell}.\n\\]\nFor active \\(j=0\\), the factor \\(-N(p)^{-1}\\) combines with a cubic Gauss sum to give \\(N(p)^{-1/2}\\chi_p(\\lambda^4\\ell)^{-2}\\). An inactive prime contributes \\(1-N(p)^{-1}\\).</p><p>It remains to calculate the archimedean transform. For \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;1\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathcal T(s,\\Psi)\n=\n\\left(\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\(n,S)=1}}^{*}\n\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\gamma_2(n)\\Psi(n)N(n)^{-s}\n\\right)\n\\left(\n\\sum_{\\substack{b\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\(b,S)=1}}\n\\overline{\\alpha(b)}^{\\,3}\\Psi(b)^3N(b)^{-3s+1/2}\n\\right).\n\\]\nMellin inversion gives\n\\[\nT(X;\\Psi)\n=\n\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_{(\\sigma)}\n\\widehat V_*(s-\\tfrac12)\n\\mathcal T(s,\\Psi)X^{s-1/2}\\,ds,\n\\qquad \\sigma&gt;1.\n\\tag{A.13}\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\mathcal J(s)\n=\n\\int_0^\\infty\n\\left.\\partial_{\\overline z}\\Theta_\\Psi(z,v)\\right|_{z=0}\nv^{2s-1}\\,dv.\n\\]\nFor each mode,\n\\[\n\\partial_{\\overline z}\\check e(\\ell z)\n=\n2\\pi i\\overline\\ell\\,\\check e(\\ell z),\n\\tag{A.14}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty v^{2s}K_{1/3}(4\\pi|\\ell|v)\\,dv\n=\n\\frac{2^{2s-1}\\Gamma(s+1/3)\\Gamma(s+2/3)}\n{(4\\pi|\\ell|)^{2s+1}}.\n\\tag{A.15}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell=\\lambda^{-3}nb^3\\),\n\\[\ni\\overline{\\alpha(\\ell)}|\\ell|^{-2s}\n=\n27^s\\overline{\\alpha(n)}\\,\n\\overline{\\alpha(b)}^{\\,3}\nN(n)^{-s}N(b)^{-3s}.\n\\]\nAbsolute convergence permits termwise integration for \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;1\\), giving\n\\[\n\\mathcal J(s)\n=\n\\frac{3^{5/2}}4\n\\left(\\frac{27}{(2\\pi)^2}\\right)^s\n\\Gamma(s+1/3)\\Gamma(s+2/3)\\mathcal T(s,\\Psi).\n\\tag{A.16}\n\\]</p><p>Differentiating (A.9) at \\(z=0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial z'}{\\partial\\overline z}\\right|_{z=0}\n=-\\frac1{c^2v^2},\n\\qquad\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial\\overline z'}{\\partial\\overline z}\\right|_{z=0}\n=\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial v'}{\\partial\\overline z}\\right|_{z=0}=0.\n\\]\nThus the derivative in cusp coordinates is \\(-(cv)^{-2}\\partial_{z'}\\), with no height-derivative term. Horizontal differentiation removes the constant Fourier mode. The Fourier series gives exponential decay at infinity, and the transformed cusp height \\(1/(N(c)v)\\) gives exponential decay in \\(1/v\\) at zero. Hence \\(\\mathcal J(s)\\), and therefore \\(\\mathcal T(s,\\Psi)\\), continue to entire functions.</p><p>For each translate, define\n\\[\n\\mathcal J_{\\mathbf h}^{\\vee}(s)\n=\n\\int_0^\\infty\n\\left.\n\\partial_z\\{\\overline{\\theta(H_{\\mathbf h}(z,v))}\\}\n\\right|_{z=-\\delta_{\\mathbf h}'/c_{\\mathbf h}}\nv^{2s-1}\\,dv.\n\\tag{A.17}\n\\]\nSubstitution \\(v\\mapsto(N(c_{\\mathbf h})v)^{-1}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal J(s)\n=\n-\\sum_{\\mathbf h}\nc_F(\\mathbf h)\\overline{\\kappa(g_{1,\\mathbf h})}\n\\overline{\\alpha(c_{\\mathbf h})}^{\\,2}\nN(c_{\\mathbf h})^{1-2s}\n\\mathcal J_{\\mathbf h}^{\\vee}(1-s).\n\\tag{A.18}\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n\\mathcal J_{\\mathbf h}^{\\vee}(s)\n=\n\\frac{i\\Gamma(s+1/3)\\Gamma(s+2/3)}\n{4(2\\pi)^{2s}}\n\\sum_{0\\ne\\ell\\in\\lambda^{-4}\\mathcal O}\n\\frac{d_{\\sigma_{\\mathbf h}}(\\ell)\\alpha(\\ell)}\n{N(\\ell)^s}\n\\check e(-\\delta_{\\mathbf h}'\\ell/c_{\\mathbf h}).\n\\tag{A.19}\n\\]</p><p>The series on the original side converges absolutely for \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&gt;1\\), and the dual series at \\(1-s\\) for \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&lt;0\\). The functional equation, Stirling’s formula, finite order from the split Mellin integral, and Phragmén–Lindelöf give polynomial vertical bounds. These and the rapid decay of \\(\\widehat V_*\\) justify moving the contour in (A.13) to \\(\\operatorname{Re}s&lt;0\\), crossing no poles.</p><p>Set \\(t=\\tfrac12-s\\), insert (A.19), and apply (A.12). The scalar of a group of translates is\n\\[\nC=\n-\\frac i{81}\\overline{\\alpha(c)}^{\\,2}\n\\widehat\\phi(h_0)\\overline{\\kappa_0}\n\\prod_{p\\ \\mathrm{inactive}}(1-N(p)^{-1})\n\\prod_{p\\ \\mathrm{active}}\\chi_p(\\sigma_p)^{-2}\\omega_{p,j_p},\n\\]\nso \\(|C|\\le1/81\\). Dividing by the gamma factors in (A.16) gives the quotient in (6.6). No numerator pole lies between the resulting line \\(\\operatorname{Re}t&gt;1/2\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Re}t=0\\), so shifting each kernel contour to zero proves (6.9).</p><p>Finally, in the family of the uniformity assertion,\n\\[\nr=k_0\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{fix}}}p.\n\\]\nFixing \\(k_0\\bmod M^2\\) fixes \\(r\\bmod M^2\\), and therefore fixes \\(H,c_0\\), and the residues defining \\(\\psi\\). Thus \\(d,\\psi,c_0\\) have precisely the asserted ray class dependence. Lemma 7.2 supplies the coefficient and weight estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( T(X;k,f) = \\frac1{3^{5/2}\\sqrt X} \\sum_{\\substack{n,b\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\(nb,S)=1}} c_\\theta(nb^3)\\phi_k(nb^3) \\overline{\\alpha(nb^3)}W(N(nb^3)/X), \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and theta function of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a ray class character \\(\\xi\\), a nonzero \\(k\\in\\mathcal O\\), and a squarefree primary \\(f\\) prime to \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\Psi_k(n)=\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4\n\\]\nfor \\((n,S)=1\\), with zero extension otherwise, and write \\(T(X;k,f)=T(X;\\Psi_k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c_\\theta(nb^3)\\) be the coefficient of \\(\\overline\\theta\\) at \\(\\lambda^{-3}nb^3\\). Patterson’s formula is\n\\[\nc_\\theta(nb^3)\n=\n3^{5/2}|b|\\,\n\\overline{\\chi_n(\\lambda)^2}\\gamma_2(n),\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nfor squarefree primary \\(n\\), primary \\(b\\), and \\((nb,6)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\phi_k(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\chi_n(\\lambda)^2\\Psi_k(n),&amp;\nn\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\ (n,S)=1,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Theta_k(z,v)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\ell\\in\\lambda^{-3}\\mathcal O\\\\\\ell\\ne0}}\n\\overline{\\tau(-\\ell)}\\phi_k(\\lambda^3\\ell)\nvK_{1/3}(4\\pi|\\ell|v)\n\\exp(2\\pi i(\\ell z+\\overline{\\ell z})).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(q\\ne0\\) for which \\(\\phi_k\\) is periodic modulo \\(q\\), and put\n\\[\n\\widehat\\phi_k(h)\n=\n\\frac1{N(q)}\\sum_{n\\bmod q}\\phi_k(n)e(-hn/q).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nT(X;k,f)\n=\n\\frac1{3^{5/2}\\sqrt X}\n\\sum_{\\substack{n,b\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\(nb,S)=1}}\nc_\\theta(nb^3)\\phi_k(nb^3)\n\\overline{\\alpha(nb^3)}W(N(nb^3)/X),\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Theta_k(z,v)\n=\n\\sum_{h\\in\\mathcal O/(q)}\n\\widehat\\phi_k(h)\\,\n\\overline{\\theta(z+\\lambda^2h/q,v)}.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The coefficient formula gives\n\\[\nc_\\theta(nb^3)\\phi_k(nb^3)\n=\n3^{5/2}|b|\\gamma_2(n)\\Psi_k(n)\\Psi_k(b)^3.\n\\]\nSubstitution into (5.3), using \\(V_*(y)=\\sqrt y\\,W(y)\\), proves (6.3).</p><p>Finite Fourier inversion gives\n\\[\n\\phi_k(n)=\\sum_{h\\bmod q}\\widehat\\phi_k(h)e(nh/q),\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{h\\bmod q}\\widehat\\phi_k(h)=\\phi_k(0)=0.\n\\]\nMultiplication of the \\(\\ell\\)-th Fourier mode by \\(e(\\lambda^3\\ell h/q)\\) translates \\(z\\) to \\(z+\\lambda^2h/q\\). The second identity cancels the constant term, proving (6.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The coefficient \\(d(\\ell)\\) vanishes unless \\( \\ell=u\\lambda^mnb^3, \\) where \\(u\\in\\mathcal O^\\times\\), \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\(m\\ge-4\\), and \\(n,b\\) are primary with \\(n\\) squarefree.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the cusp coefficients and transformed weight of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\in\\{d_0,d_+,d_-\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficient \\(d(\\ell)\\) vanishes unless\n\\[\n\\ell=u\\lambda^mnb^3,\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nwhere \\(u\\in\\mathcal O^\\times\\), \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\(m\\ge-4\\), and \\(n,b\\) are primary with \\(n\\) squarefree. For these indices,\n\\[\n|d(u\\lambda^mnb^3)|\n\\le27\\cdot3^{m/6}|b|.\n\\tag{6.11}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(A&gt;0\\), integer \\(j\\ge0\\), and \\(V_*\\) supported in a fixed compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\), there is \\(J=J(A,j)\\) such that\n\\[\n|(x\\partial_x)^jV_*^\\sharp(x)|\n\\ll_{A,j,I}\n\\|V_*\\|_{C^J(I)}\n\\min(x^{1/4},x^{-A}),\n\\qquad x&gt;0.\n\\tag{6.12}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(t_\\sigma(\\ell)\\) denote the coefficient of the corresponding Fourier mode in \\(\\theta(\\gamma_\\sigma(z,v))\\). Patterson’s cusp calculation, in the notation of Dunn–Radziwiłł, gives\n\\[\nt_0(\\ell)=\\tau(\\ell),\n\\]\n\\[\nt_-(\\ell)=\\omega^2\\tau_1(\\omega^2\\ell)\\check e(\\ell),\n\\qquad\nt_+(\\ell)=\\omega\\tau_2(\\omega\\ell)\\check e(\\ell),\n\\tag{A.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\check e(z)=\\exp(2\\pi i(z+\\overline z))\\), and\n\\[\nd_\\sigma(\\ell)=\\overline{t_\\sigma(-\\ell)}.\n\\tag{A.7}\n\\]\nFor indices \\(\\ell=u\\lambda^mnb^3\\), the coefficient magnitudes satisfy\n\\[\n|t_0(\\ell)|\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3^{k/2+2}|b|,&amp;m=3k-4,\\ k\\ge1,\\\\\n3^{k/2+5/2}|b|,&amp;m=3k-3,\\ k\\ge0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe \\(m=-4\\) coefficients of \\(t_-\\) and \\(t_+\\) have magnitude at most \\(9|b|\\). These explicit coefficient formulas give (6.10) and (6.11).</p><p>For the weight, the first numerator pole of the gamma quotient in (6.6) is at \\(t=-5/6\\). Shift the contour to \\(\\operatorname{Re}t=-1/4\\) for \\(0&lt;x\\le1\\), and to \\(\\operatorname{Re}t=A\\) for \\(x\\ge1\\). These shifts give \\(x^{1/4}\\) and \\(x^{-A}\\), respectively. Each application of \\(x\\partial_x\\) introduces a factor \\(-t\\).</p><p>Stirling’s formula gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|(x\\partial_x)^jV_*^\\sharp(x)|\n&amp;\\ll_{A,j}\\min\\{x^{1/4},(1+x)^{-A}\\}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\times\n\\sup_{-A\\le\\eta\\le1/4}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n(1+|u|)^{\\lceil4A\\rceil+j+2}\n|\\widehat V_*(\\eta+iu)|\\,du.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(A.20)\n\\]\nRepeated integration by parts in the Mellin transform bounds the last expression by \\(\\|V_*\\|_{C^J(I)}\\) for sufficiently large \\(J=J(A,j)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\ge1\\) such that, for every compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) and every smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arithmetic setting of Proposition 4 and the completed sums of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), \\(C_0\\ge1\\), and a character \\(\\xi\\) of the fixed ray class group.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\ge2\\), let \\(f\\) be squarefree and primary with \\((f,S)=1\\), and suppose\n\\[\n1\\le\\mathcal H,X,N(f)\\le D^{C_0}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k\\ne0\\), define\n\\[\n\\Psi_k(n)=\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4\n\\]\naway from \\(S\\), with zero extension at \\(S\\), and put \\(T(X;k,f)=T(X;\\Psi_k)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\ge1\\) such that, for every compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) and every smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(k)\\ll\\mathcal H}|T(X;k,f)|^2\n\\ll_{I,\\xi,S,\\varepsilon,C_0}\nD^\\varepsilon\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2\n\\left(\\mathcal H+\\frac{\\mathcal H^2N(f)}X\\right).\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write uniquely\n\\[\nk=u_0sv^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(s\\) is squarefree and \\(s,v\\) use the chosen ideal generators; no condition \\((s,v)=1\\) is imposed. Since \\(8\\equiv2\\pmod6\\), including the zero extensions,\n\\[\n\\chi_n(u_0sv^2)\\chi_n(f)^4\n=\n\\chi_n(u_0s)\\chi_n(fv^2)^4,\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\nT(X;u_0sv^2,f)=T(X;u_0s,fv^2).\n\\]</p><p>Apply Lemma 8.2 with row bound \\(\\mathcal H/N(v)^2\\) and \\(g=fv^2\\). Since\n\\[\nN(g)\\le\\mathcal HN(f)\\le D^{2C_0},\n\\]\nuse that lemma with \\(2C_0\\). Summing gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(k)\\le\\mathcal H}|T(X;k,f)|^2\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{u_0\\in\\mathcal O^\\times}\n\\sum_{N(v)^2\\le\\mathcal H}\n\\sum_{\\substack{N(s)\\le\\mathcal H/N(v)^2\\\\s\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}}}\n|T(X;u_0s,fv^2)|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\nD^\\varepsilon\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2\n\\left(\\mathcal H+\\frac{\\mathcal H^2N(f)}X\\right)\n\\sum_v\\frac1{N(v)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last sum is \\(\\zeta_K(2)&lt;\\infty\\), proving (5.5).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over \\(\\mathcal O=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\), with \\(S\\) fixed as in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H,U\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\beta(n)\\) be complex coefficients supported on squarefree primary \\(n\\) with \\(N(n)\\asymp U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{k\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\(k,S)=1\\\\N(k)\\le H}}^{*}\n\\left|\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\N(n)\\asymp U}}\n\\beta(n)\\chi_k(n)^3\n\\right|^2\n\\ll_{S,\\varepsilon}\n(HU)^\\varepsilon(H+U)\\sum_n|\\beta(n)|^2.\n\\tag{6.13}\n\\]\nThe star restricts \\(k\\) to squarefree primary elements.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Goldmakher–Louvel’s quadratic large sieve, after fixing the product of prime factors of \\(n\\) lying in \\(S\\), and finitely many ray classes.</p><p>For completeness, let \\(e_k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) according as \\(N(k)\\equiv1,3\\pmod4\\), and let \\(\\kappa_\\lambda\\) be the nontrivial character modulo \\(\\lambda\\). The character\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto(x/k)_2\\kappa_\\lambda(x)^{e_k}\n\\]\nis trivial on units and has primitive conductor \\(k\\lambda^{e_k}\\). When \\(e_k=0\\), omit the supplementary factor. Classes modulo \\(24\\mathcal O\\) fix the supplementary characters and reciprocity factors; for coprime \\(k_1,k_2\\) in the same class, the product character has conductor \\(k_1k_2\\). These are the hypotheses of the cited quadratic large sieve.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there exists \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\ge1\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the setting of Proposition 8, but replace \\(f\\) by an arbitrary nonzero \\(g\\in\\mathcal O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Thus the twist is\n\\[\n\\Psi_k(n)=\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(g)^4,\n\\]\nwith zero extension at \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose prime-ideal generators, primary away from \\(3\\), and extend the choice multiplicatively to all ideals.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_0\\in\\mathcal O^\\times\\), and suppose\n\\[\n1\\le\\mathcal H,X,N(g)\\le D^{C_0}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">The squarefree row sum uses the chosen generators, including those meeting \\(S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there exists \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\ge1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{0&lt;N(s)\\le\\mathcal H\\\\s\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}}}\n|T(X;u_0s,g)|^2\n\\ll_{I,\\xi,S,\\varepsilon,C_0}\nD^\\varepsilon\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2\n\\left(\\mathcal H+\\frac{\\mathcal H^2N(g)}X\\right).\n\\tag{6.14}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By homogeneity assume \\(\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}\\le1\\). Write\n\\[\ns=tk_0,\n\\qquad\nt=\\prod_{\\substack{p\\mid s\\\\p\\mid g\\ \\mathrm{or}\\ p\\in S}}p,\n\\qquad\nN(k_0)\\le H_0:=\\frac{\\mathcal H}{N(t)}.\n\\]\nFix \\(t\\), discard empty ranges, and retain squarefreeness and\n\\[\n(k_0,g)=(k_0,S)=1.\n\\]</p><p>For a coefficient function \\(A(n,b)\\), a positive scale \\(Y\\), and \\(m\\ge-4\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_m[A,Y](k_0)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{n,b\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}}}\n\\frac{A(n,b)\\chi_{k_0}(nb)^3}\n{3^{m/3}\\sqrt{N(n)}\\,N(b)}\nV_*^\\sharp\\!\\left(\n\\frac{3^mN(n)N(b)^3H_0^2}{YN(k_0)^2}\n\\right).\n\\tag{6.15}\n\\]\nAfter partitioning \\(k_0\\) into fixed ray classes, we obtain\n\\[\nT(X;u_0tk_0,g)\n=\n\\sum_{\\iota\\in\\mathcal I}\\sum_{m\\ge-4}\nc_{\\iota,m}(k_0)\\mathscr S_m[A_{\\iota,m},Y_\\iota](k_0),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal I\\) is independent of \\(k_0\\), divisor-bounded in \\(tg\\), the scalars are bounded, the \\(A_{\\iota,m}\\) are independent of \\(k_0\\), and\n\\[\n|A_{\\iota,m}(n,b)|\\ll a_\\iota,\n\\qquad\na_\\iota^2\\le1,\n\\qquad\na_\\iota^2Y_\\iota\n\\ll\\frac{H_0^2N(t)^2N(g)}X.\n\\tag{6.16}\n\\]</p><p>To obtain this representation, put \\(k=u_0tk_0\\), let\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{p\\notin S:p\\mid kg\\},\n\\]\nand use sextic reciprocity:\n\\[\n\\Psi_k(n)=\\Psi_0(n)\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\chi_p(n)^{j_p},\n\\qquad\nj_p\\equiv v_p(k)+4v_p(g)\\pmod6.\n\\tag{6.17}\n\\]\nThe exponents are chosen in \\(\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}\\); \\(j_p=0\\) retains the zero extension at \\(p\\). The fixed factor \\(\\Psi_0\\) contains \\(\\xi\\), the factors at \\(S\\), unit factors, and the reciprocity factor\n\\[\nn\\longmapsto\n\\mathcal R\\!\\left(n,\\prod_{p\\notin S}p^{v_p(k)}\\right).\n\\]\nThe fourth power at \\(g\\) contributes no reciprocity sign. These fixed factors range over a finite family.</p><p>Take \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{fix}}=\\{p\\notin S:p\\mid tg\\}\\). Proposition 7, including its uniformity assertion, fixes the cusp data in each ray class of \\(k_0\\). For one transformed term, put\n\\[\nc_*=c_0\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{fix}}}p,\n\\qquad c=c_*k_0.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\ell=u\\lambda^mnb^3\\). Every prime dividing \\(k_0\\) has exponent \\(j_p=1\\), so\n\\[\nB_{p,1}(u\\lambda^{m+4}nb^3)\n=\n\\chi_p(u\\lambda^{m+4})^3\\chi_p(nb)^3.\n\\tag{6.18}\n\\]\nThis remains valid with zero extensions.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA_{u,m}(n,b)=\n\\frac{\nd(u\\lambda^mnb^3)\\alpha(u\\lambda^mnb^3)\n\\psi(u\\lambda^{m+4}nb^3)}\n{3^{m/6}\\sqrt{N(b)}}.\n\\]\nThe coefficient bound gives \\(|A_{u,m}(n,b)|\\le27\\). The transformed term is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(k_0)\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal O^\\times}\\sum_{m\\ge-4}\n\\chi_{k_0}(u\\lambda^{m+4})^3\n\\sum_{\\substack{n,b\\equiv1\\ (3)\\\\n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}}}\n&amp;\\frac{A_{u,m}(n,b)\\chi_{k_0}(nb)^3}\n{3^{m/3}\\sqrt{N(n)}\\,N(b)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{fix}}}\nB_{p,j_p}(u\\lambda^{m+4}nb^3)\n&amp;V_*^\\sharp\\!\\left(\n\\frac{3^mN(n)N(b)^3X}{N(c_*)^2N(k_0)^2}\n\\right),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwith \\(|C(k_0)|\\ll1\\). Absolute convergence follows from the coefficient bounds and rapid decay. The representation of \\(\\ell\\) is unique, since away from \\(\\lambda\\) its prime exponents are \\(3v_p(b)\\) or \\(1+3v_p(b)\\).</p><p>For an active \\(p\\nmid k_0\\), put \\(q=N(p)\\). When \\(j_p=4\\),\n\\[\nB_{p,4}(u\\lambda^{m+4}nb^3)\n=\n-q^{-1/2}\n+q^{1/2}1_{p\\mid n}\n+q^{1/2}1_{p\\nmid n,\\ p\\mid b}.\n\\]\nLet \\(A^{(p)}\\) include the other local factors and set\n\\[\nA^{(p,n)}(n,b)=1_{p\\nmid n}A^{(p)}(pn,b),\n\\qquad\nA^{(p,b)}(n,b)=1_{p\\nmid n}A^{(p)}(n,pb).\n\\]\nThe changes \\(n=pn'\\), \\(b=pb'\\) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathscr S_m[A^{(p)}B_{p,4},q^2Y](k_0)\n={}&amp;-q^{-1/2}\\mathscr S_m[A^{(p)},q^2Y](k_0)\\\\\n&amp;+\\chi_{k_0}(p)^3\\mathscr S_m[A^{(p,n)},qY](k_0)\\\\\n&amp;+q^{-1/2}\\chi_{k_0}(p)^3\n\\mathscr S_m[A^{(p,b)},Y/q](k_0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe restriction \\(p\\nmid n'\\) preserves squarefreeness; in the last term \\(b'\\) is unrestricted at \\(p\\).</p><p>Track \\(|A(n,b)|\\le27a\\) and the scale \\(Y\\). Initially,\n\\[\na=1,\\qquad Y=N(c_0)^2H_0^2/X.\n\\]\nIncluding the conductor factor \\(q^2\\), the local updates are\n\\[\n(a^2,Y)\\longmapsto\n\\begin{cases}\n(a^2,q^2Y),&amp;j_p\\notin\\{0,4\\},\\\\\n(a^2/q,q^2Y),&amp;j_p=0,\\\\\n(a^2/q,q^2Y),\\ (a^2,qY),\\ (a^2/q,Y/q),&amp;j_p=4.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus \\(a^2\\) never increases. If \\(p\\mid t\\), its exponent is odd and costs at most \\(N(p)^2\\). If \\(p\\mid g\\), \\(p\\nmid t\\), the cost in \\(a^2Y\\) is \\(N(p)\\) for \\(j_p=0,4\\), and \\(N(p)^2\\) for \\(j_p=2\\); the latter requires \\(v_p(g)\\ge2\\). Hence\n\\[\na^2Y\n\\ll\n\\frac{H_0^2}{X}\n\\prod_{\\substack{p\\mid t\\\\p\\notin S}}N(p)^2\n\\prod_{\\substack{p\\mid g\\\\p\\nmid t,\\ p\\notin S}}N(p)^{v_p(g)}\n\\le\n\\frac{H_0^2N(t)^2N(g)}X.\n\\]\nThis proves (6.16), with divisor-boundedly many choices. Also \\(Y\\ll D^{C_1}\\) for a fixed \\(C_1=C_1(C_0)\\).</p><p>Fix one such choice, write \\(a,Y,A_m\\), split \\(b\\) into \\(B\\le N(b)&lt;2B\\), and split \\(n\\) smoothly at dyadic \\(U\\ge1\\). The corresponding part is\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_{m;U,B}(k_0)\n=\n3^{-m/3}\\sum_{B\\le N(b)&lt;2B}\n\\frac{\\chi_{k_0}(b)^3}{N(b)}F_b(k_0),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nF_b(k_0)\n=\n\\sum_{n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}}\n\\frac{A_m(n,b)\\chi_{k_0}(n)^3}{\\sqrt{N(n)}}\nV(N(n)/U)\nV_*^\\sharp\\!\\left(\n\\frac{3^mN(n)N(b)^3H_0^2}{YN(k_0)^2}\n\\right).\n\\]\nPut \\(z_*=3^mUB^3/Y\\). Proposition 1 separates only \\(N(n)/U\\):\n\\[\nF_b(k_0)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}c_{b,k_0}(s)G_{b,s}(k_0)\\,ds,\n\\]\n\\[\nG_{b,s}(k_0)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\N(n)\\asymp U}}\n\\frac{A_m(n,b)\\chi_{k_0}(n)^3}{\\sqrt{N(n)}}\n(N(n)/U)^{is},\n\\tag{6.19}\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n|c_{b,k_0}(s)|\n\\ll_{I,A}(1+z_*)^{-A}(1+|s|)^{-2}.\n\\]\nThe transformed-weight derivative bounds in Proposition 7 make this uniform.</p><p>Since \\(\\sum_{N(n)\\asymp U}N(n)^{-1}\\ll1\\), Lemma 8.1 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{N(k_0)\\le H_0}^{*}|G_{b,s}(k_0)|^2\n\\ll_{S,\\sigma}\na^2(H_0U)^\\sigma(H_0+U).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 3 to the Mellin integral, then weighted Cauchy–Schwarz in \\(b\\), using\n\\[\n\\sum_{B\\le N(b)&lt;2B}N(b)^{-1}\\ll1.\n\\]\nThis gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{N(k_0)\\le H_0}^{*}\n|\\mathscr S_{m;U,B}(k_0)|^2\n\\ll_{I,S,A,\\sigma}\na^2\\,3^{-2m/3}(H_0U)^\\sigma(H_0+U)\n(1+z_*)^{-2A}.\n\\tag{6.20}\n\\]\nSince \\(m\\ge-4\\), \\(U\\le81Yz_*\\). Choose \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\) sufficiently small in terms of a requested loss \\(\\varepsilon_1\\), and take \\(A=3\\). Taking square roots gives\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\sum_{N(k_0)\\le H_0}^{*}\n|\\mathscr S_{m;U,B}(k_0)|^2\n\\right)^{1/2}\n\\ll\naD^{\\varepsilon_1}3^{-m/3}\n(\\sqrt{H_0}+\\sqrt U)\n(1+3^mUB^3/Y)^{-2}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(Z&gt;0\\), the dyadic sums satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_{U,B}(1+UB^3/Z)^{-2}\\ll\\log^2(2+Z),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{U,B}\\sqrt U(1+UB^3/Z)^{-2}\\ll\\sqrt Z.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality in \\(\\ell^2(k_0)\\), followed by the geometric sum over \\(m\\ge-4\\), therefore gives\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\sum_{N(k_0)\\le H_0}^{*}\n\\left|\\sum_{m\\ge-4}c_{\\iota,m}(k_0)\n\\mathscr S_m[A_m,Y](k_0)\\right|^2\n\\right)^{1/2}\n\\ll\naD^{\\varepsilon_1}\n\\left(\\sqrt{H_0}\\log^2(2+Y)+\\sqrt Y\\right).\n\\]</p><p>Combining the divisor-bounded choices and (6.16),\n\\[\n\\sum_{N(k_0)\\le H_0}^{*}\n|T(X;u_0tk_0,g)|^2\n\\preccurlyeq\nH_0+\\frac{H_0^2N(t)^2N(g)}X\n\\le\n\\mathcal H+\\frac{\\mathcal H^2N(g)}X.\n\\]\nHere and below \\(A\\preccurlyeq B\\) means \\(A\\ll_\\eta D^\\eta B\\) for every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), uniformly over the stated parameter ranges.</p><p>Sum the fixed ray classes and the divisor-bounded choices\n\\[\nt\\mid\\operatorname{rad}\\!\\left(g\\prod_{p\\in S}p\\right),\n\\]\nallocating the small-power losses within \\(\\varepsilon\\). The weight estimates require finitely many derivatives \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\). Homogeneity restores \\(\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(m\\ge0\\) and every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), \\( \\mathcal A(W) \\ll D^\\varepsilon\\Sigma\\|W\\|_{C^{4m+12}(I)}^2 \\left( 1+\\sup \\frac{\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H',X',F';\\xi',U)}{\\Sigma'} \\right), \\) where …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arithmetic setting and the family \\(\\mathcal E\\) of Propositions 4 and 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a nonnegative radial Schwartz weight \\(\\Phi\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Phi(t)\\ge1\\quad(0\\le t\\le1),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat\\Phi\\subset[0,C_\\Phi].\n\\]\nSuch a weight is obtained by squaring and rescaling a real radial Schwartz function with compactly supported Fourier transform.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(C_0\\ge1\\), \\(D\\ge2\\), and suppose\n\\[\n1\\le\\mathcal H,L,F,\\Sigma\\le D^{C_0},\n\\qquad\n\\max\\{\\mathcal H,LF\\}\\le\\Sigma.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\Sigma\\) is an independent target scale.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(W\\in C_c^\\infty(I)\\), \\(I=[u,v]\\subset(0,\\infty)\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathcal A(W)\n=\n\\frac1{LF}\n\\sum_{\\substack{F\\le N(f)&lt;2F\\\\(f,S)=1}}^{*}\n\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal O}\\Phi(N(k)/\\mathcal H)\n\\left|\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4W(N(n)/L)\n\\right|^2.\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(m\\ge0\\) and every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal A(W)\n\\ll\nD^\\varepsilon\\Sigma\\|W\\|_{C^{4m+12}(I)}^2\n\\left(\n1+\\sup\n\\frac{\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H',X',F';\\xi',U)}{\\Sigma'}\n\\right),\n\\tag{5.11}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Sigma'=X'F'\\), the supremum is over the family in Proposition 6 with\n\\[\n\\mathcal H',X',F'\\ge1,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H'\\le\\frac{\\mathcal HL}{\\Sigma F},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\mathcal H'}{\\Sigma'}\\le\\frac{\\mathcal H}{\\Sigma},\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma'\\le L,\n\\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nU\\in C_c^\\infty(I'),\\qquad\nI'=[u/16,4v],\\qquad\n\\|U\\|_{C^m(I')}\\le1.\n\\]\nAn empty supremum is zero. The implied constant depends on \\(m,C_0,\\varepsilon,I,\\nu,S\\), and the fixed ray class group. Also,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,L,F;\\xi,W)\\le\\mathcal A(W).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(S_m\\) be the supremum in (5.11). Lemma 9.3 with loss \\(D^{\\varepsilon/4}\\) supplies the hypothesis of Lemma 9.1 with\n\\[\nj=2m+4,\n\\qquad\nM\\ll D^{\\varepsilon/4}\\Sigma(1+S_m).\n\\]\nApply Lemma 9.1 with the same loss. This gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal A(W)\n\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}\\Sigma(1+S_m)\n\\|W\\|_{C^{4m+12}(I)}^2,\n\\]\nwhich proves (5.11). The domination of \\(\\mathcal E\\) by \\(\\mathcal A\\) follows from \\(\\Phi\\ge1\\) on \\([0,1]\\) and its nonnegativity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal A(W) \\ll D^{\\varepsilon_0}(\\Sigma+M) \\|W\\|_{C^{2j+4}(I)}^2. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the setting of Proposition 9, and put \\(I_*=[u/2,2v]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For coprime squarefree primary \\(C,t\\) prime to \\(S\\), and \\(d\\mid C\\), put\n\\[\n\\ell=\\frac L{N(C)N(t)},\n\\qquad\nw_{C,d}=\\frac{\\mathcal HN(C)}{N(d)L^2F},\n\\qquad\nY_{C,d}=c_I\\frac{\\Sigma LF N(d)}{\\mathcal HN(C)^2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_I\\ge\\max(1,4C_\\Phi v^2)\\) is fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Retain only \\(N(C)N(t)\\le2vL\\), equivalently \\(\\ell\\ge1/(2v)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed ray class character \\(\\xi_1\\), define\n\\[\np_y(n)\n=\n\\mu(n)\\xi_1(n)1_{(n,t)=1}\n\\overline{\\chi_n(y)}\\chi_n(C)^4\\chi_n(d),\n\\]\n\\[\nP_{C,d,t}(y;U)=\\sum_n^{*}p_y(n)U(N(n)/\\ell),\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{\\xi_1}(U)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{C,t\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\(Ct,S)=1,\\ (C,t)=1\\\\N(C)N(t)\\le2vL}}\n\\sum_{d\\mid C}w_{C,d}\n\\sum_{y\\in\\mathcal O}\n\\Phi(N(y)/Y_{C,d})|P_{C,d,t}(y;U)|^2.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nThe scales \\(Y_{C,d}\\) may be smaller than \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(j\\ge0\\), \\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\), and suppose \\(M\\ge0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{\\xi_1}(U)\\le M\\|U\\|_{C^j(I_*)}^2\n\\]\nfor every \\(U\\in C_c^\\infty(I_*)\\) and every \\(\\xi_1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal A(W)\n\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon_0}(\\Sigma+M)\n\\|W\\|_{C^{2j+4}(I)}^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the square defining \\(\\mathcal A(W)\\), and write\n\\[\nn_i=Cu_i,\\qquad C=(n_1,n_2),\n\\qquad\n(u_1,u_2)=(u_1u_2,C)=1.\n\\]\nThe row character is \\(\\chi_{u_1}\\overline{\\chi_{u_2}}\\), primitive modulo \\(u_1u_2\\), with the extra restriction \\((k,C)=1\\). Proposition 5 introduces \\(d\\mid C\\) and a Fourier frequency \\(h\\).</p><p>The zero-frequency contribution \\(Z\\) requires \\(u_1=u_2=1\\), so\n\\[\n|Z|\n\\ll\n\\frac{\\mathcal H}{LF}\n\\sum_{F\\le N(f)&lt;2F}^{*}\n\\sum_C^{*}|W(N(C)/L)|^2\n\\ll_I\\mathcal H\\|W\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\]</p><p>For the other frequencies, Proposition 4 and the Chinese remainder theorem give\n\\[\na_\\xi(u_1)\\overline{a_\\xi(u_2)}\n\\gamma(\\chi_{u_1}\\overline{\\chi_{u_2}})\n=\n\\mu(u_1)\\mu(u_2)(\\xi G)(u_1u_2^{-1}).\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nExpand\n\\[\n(\\xi G)(z)=\\sum_{\\xi_1}\\ell_{\\xi_1}\\xi_1(z).\n\\]\nInsert\n\\[\n1_{(u_1,u_2)=1}\n=\n\\sum_{t\\mid u_1,\\ t\\mid u_2}\\mu(t),\n\\qquad u_i=tx_i.\n\\]\nSquarefreeness imposes \\((x_i,t)=1\\), but no \\((x_1,x_2)=1\\) remains. The factors at \\(C,t\\) cancel against their conjugates, leaving \\(\\mu(d)\\mu(t)\\) and \\((f,Ct)=(h,t)=1\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A(W)-Z\n={}&amp;\n\\sum_{\\xi_1}\\ell_{\\xi_1}\n\\sum_{\\substack{C,t\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\(Ct,S)=1,\\ (C,t)=1\\\\N(C)N(t)\\le2vL}}\n\\sum_{d\\mid C}\\mu(d)\\mu(t)w_{C,d}\\\\\n&amp;\\times\n\\sum_{\\substack{F\\le N(f)&lt;2F\\\\(f,Ct)=1}}^{*}\n\\sum_{\\substack{h\\ne0,\\ (h,t)=1\\\\\nN(h)\\le C_\\Phi v^2L^2N(d)/(\\mathcal HN(C)^2)}}\n\\sum_{x_1,x_2}^{*}\np_{hf^2}(x_1)\\overline{p_{hf^2}(x_2)}\n\\mathcal K_h(N(x_1)/\\ell,N(x_2)/\\ell),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_h(z_1,z_2)\n=\n(z_1z_2)^{-1/2}W(z_1)\\overline{W(z_2)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{\\mathcal HN(h)N(C)^2}\n{L^2N(d)z_1z_2}\n\\right).\n\\]\nFor every \\(j_0\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{C,d,h}\\|\\mathcal K_h\\|_{C^{j_0}(I^2)}\n\\ll_{I,\\Phi,j_0}\\|W\\|_{C^{j_0}(I)}^2.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(C,d,t\\), the map \\((f,h)\\mapsto y=hf^2\\) has divisor-bounded multiplicity. Since \\(\\Sigma/(LF)\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nN(y)\n\\le\n\\frac{4C_\\Phi v^2L^2F^2N(d)}\n{\\mathcal HN(C)^2}\n\\le Y_{C,d}.\n\\]\nNonnegativity of \\(\\Phi\\) and \\(\\Phi\\ge1\\) on \\([0,1]\\) therefore give\n\\[\n\\sum_{C,t}\\sum_{d\\mid C}w_{C,d}\n\\sum_{f,h}|P_{C,d,t}(hf^2;U)|^2\n\\preccurlyeq\n\\mathcal Q_{\\xi_1}(U)\n\\le M\\|U\\|_{C^j(I_*)}^2.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 2 to the full expression for \\(\\mathcal A(W)-Z\\). Its kernel bound gives\n\\[\n|\\mathcal A(W)-Z|\n\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon_0/2}M\\|W\\|_{C^{2j+4}(I)}^2.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(\\mathcal H\\le\\Sigma\\), and allocation of the divisor losses within \\(\\varepsilon_0\\), this proves the lemma.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal M = Z+ \\sum_{g,e,w,h,\\xi'} \\frac{Y\\mu(e)\\mu(w)c_{\\xi'}N(g)}{N(e)\\ell} \\sum_{(n_1n_2,S)=1}^{*} a^\\sharp(n_1)\\overline{a^\\sharp(n_2)} K(N(n_1)/X',N(n_2)/X'). \\) The star on the last sum restricts each column …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in the arithmetic setting of Proposition 9, but let \\(\\ell,Y&gt;0\\) be arbitrary scales.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C,t\\) be coprime squarefree primary elements prime to \\(S\\), let \\(d\\mid C\\), let \\(\\xi_1\\) be a fixed ray class character, and let \\(U\\in C_c^\\infty(I_*)\\), where \\(I_*=[a,b]\\subset(0,\\infty)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nP(y)=\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\n\\mu(n)\\xi_1(n)1_{(n,t)=1}\n\\overline{\\chi_n(y)}\\chi_n(C)^4\\chi_n(d)\nU(N(n)/\\ell),\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\sum_{y\\in\\mathcal O}\\Phi(N(y)/Y)|P(y)|^2,\n\\qquad U_0(x)=x^{-1/2}U(x).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Expand the fixed ray class function\n\\[\n\\overline{\\xi_1(z)}G(z^{-1})=\\sum_{\\xi'}c_{\\xi'}\\xi'(z).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g,w\\) be squarefree primary elements prime to \\(S\\), \\(e\\mid g\\), and \\(h\\ne0\\), subject to\n\\[\n(g,Ct)=(w,gCth)=1,\n\\qquad N(gw)\\le b\\ell,\n\\qquad\nN(h)\\le\\frac{C_\\Phi b^2\\ell^2N(e)}{YN(g)^2}.\n\\]\nFor these indices put\n\\[\nX'=\\frac{\\ell}{N(gw)},\n\\]\n\\[\na^\\sharp(n)\n=\na_{\\xi'}(n)1_{(n,tg/e)=1}\n\\chi_n(deh)\\chi_n(Cew)^4,\n\\]\n\\[\nK(x_1,x_2)\n=\nU_0(x_1)\\overline{U_0(x_2)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{YN(h)N(g)^2}{N(e)\\ell^2x_1x_2}\n\\right).\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nZ=\nY\\widehat\\Phi(0)\n\\sum_{\\substack{(g,Ct)=1\\\\(g,S)=1}}^{*}\n|U(N(g)/\\ell)|^2\n\\prod_{p\\mid g}\\left(1-\\frac1{N(p)}\\right).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal M\n=\nZ+\n\\sum_{g,e,w,h,\\xi'}\n\\frac{Y\\mu(e)\\mu(w)c_{\\xi'}N(g)}{N(e)\\ell}\n\\sum_{(n_1n_2,S)=1}^{*}\na^\\sharp(n_1)\\overline{a^\\sharp(n_2)}\nK(N(n_1)/X',N(n_2)/X').\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nThe star on the last sum restricts each column separately to squarefree primary elements. Also,\n\\[\n|Z|\\ll_{I_*,\\Phi}Y\\ell\\|U\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the square, put \\(g=(n_1,n_2)\\), and write \\(n_i=gz_i\\). Nonzero terms satisfy\n\\[\n(z_1,z_2)=(z_1z_2,gCt)=(g,Ct)=1,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\overline{\\chi_{n_1}(y)}\\chi_{n_2}(y)\n=\n1_{(y,g)=1}\\overline{\\chi_{z_1}(y)}\\chi_{z_2}(y).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 5 with exclusion \\(g\\) and divisor \\(e\\mid g\\). The zero frequency occurs exactly when \\(z_1=z_2=1\\), giving \\(Z\\); counting \\(g\\) gives its bound.</p><p>For the nonzero frequencies, Proposition 4 and the Chinese remainder theorem give\n\\[\n\\mu(z_1)\\mu(z_2)\\overline{\\xi_1(z_1)}\\xi_1(z_2)\n\\gamma(\\overline{\\chi_{z_1}}\\chi_{z_2})\n=\n\\sum_{\\xi'}c_{\\xi'}a_{\\xi'}(z_1)\\overline{a_{\\xi'}(z_2)}.\n\\]\nUse\n\\[\n\\overline{\\chi_z(dh)}\\chi_z(e)\\chi_z(C)^4\n=\n\\chi_z(deh)\\chi_z(Ce)^4\n\\]\nin the paired expression, together with\n\\[\n\\frac{U(N(gz_1)/\\ell)\\overline{U(N(gz_2)/\\ell)}}\n{\\sqrt{N(z_1)N(z_2)}}\n=\n\\frac{N(g)}{\\ell}\nU_0(N(gz_1)/\\ell)\\overline{U_0(N(gz_2)/\\ell)}.\n\\]\nThe resulting nonzero contribution is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal M-Z\n={}&amp;\n\\sum_{\\xi'}c_{\\xi'}\n\\sum_{\\substack{g\\\\(g,Ct)=1}}^{*}\n\\sum_{e\\mid g}\\frac{Y\\mu(e)N(g)}{N(e)\\ell}\n\\sum_{h\\ne0}\\\\\n&amp;\\times\n\\sum_{\\substack{z_1,z_2\\\\(z_1,z_2)=1\\\\(z_1z_2,gCt)=1}}^{*}\na_{\\xi'}(z_1)\\overline{a_{\\xi'}(z_2)}\n\\chi_{z_1}(deh)\\overline{\\chi_{z_2}(deh)}\n\\chi_{z_1}(Ce)^4\\overline{\\chi_{z_2}(Ce)^4}\\\\\n&amp;\\times\nU_0(N(gz_1)/\\ell)\\overline{U_0(N(gz_2)/\\ell)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{YN(h)}{N(e)N(z_1)N(z_2)}\n\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nInsert\n\\[\n1_{(z_1,z_2)=1}\n=\n\\sum_{w\\mid z_1,\\ w\\mid z_2}\\mu(w),\n\\qquad z_i=wn_i.\n\\]\nHere \\((w,gCt)=1\\). Equation (4.6) and zero extension give\n\\[\na_{\\xi'}(wn)=a_{\\xi'}(w)a_{\\xi'}(n)\\chi_n(w)^4\n\\]\nfor \\((w,n)=1\\), and\n\\[\n|a_{\\xi'}(w)\\chi_w(deh)\\chi_w(Ce)^4|^2\n=\n1_{(w,h)=1}.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n1_{(n,gCtw)=1}\\chi_n(deh)\\chi_n(Cew)^4\n=\n1_{(n,tg/e)=1}\\chi_n(deh)\\chi_n(Cew)^4.\n\\]\nThere is no remaining condition \\((n_1,n_2)=1\\).</p><p>The support of \\(U\\) gives \\(N(gw)\\le b\\ell\\) and\n\\[\nN(z_1z_2)\\le b^2\\ell^2/N(g)^2.\n\\]\nThe support of \\(\\widehat\\Phi\\) then imposes the stated bound on \\(N(h)\\). Substitution of (7.5) gives (7.6), with finitely many terms.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon_0>0\\), \\( \\mathcal Q_{\\xi_1}(U) \\ll D^{\\varepsilon_0}\\Sigma(1+S_m) \\|U\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I_*)}^2 \\) for every \\(U\\in C_c^\\infty(I_*)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to the full setting of Proposition 9 and Lemma 9.1, with \\(I=[u,v]\\), \\(I_*=[u/2,2v]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(m\\ge0\\), let \\(S_m\\) be the supremum in (5.11), with tests supported in \\(I'=[u/16,4v]\\) and of \\(C^m(I')\\)-norm at most one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{\\xi_1}(U)\n\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon_0}\\Sigma(1+S_m)\n\\|U\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I_*)}^2\n\\]\nfor every \\(U\\in C_c^\\infty(I_*)\\). An empty supremum is zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 9.2 with input length \\(\\ell\\), row scale \\(Y_{C,d}\\), and the same \\(C,d,t\\). For its nonzero output, set\n\\[\nr=tg/e,\\qquad f'=Cew,\\qquad k'=deh.\n\\]\nThese \\(r,f'\\) are coprime and squarefree, and\n\\[\na^\\sharp(n)\n=\na_{\\xi'}(n)1_{(n,r)=1}\\chi_n(k')\\chi_n(f')^4.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(X_0'\\) for the individual column scale in Lemma 9.2. Direct substitution gives\n\\[\nX_0'=\\frac L{N(r)N(f')},\n\\]\n\\[\nw_{C,d}\\frac{Y_{C,d}N(g)}{N(e)\\ell}\n=\n\\frac{c_I\\Sigma N(r)}{L^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{Y_{C,d}N(h)N(g)^2}{N(e)\\ell^2}\n=\n\\frac{c_I\\Sigma F N(k')N(r)^2}{\\mathcal HL}.\n\\]\nThus the kernel is\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{r,f',k'}(x_1,x_2)\n=\nU_0(x_1)\\overline{U_0(x_2)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{c_I\\Sigma F N(k')N(r)^2}\n{\\mathcal HLx_1x_2}\n\\right),\n\\]\nevaluated at \\(x_j=N(n_j)/X_0'\\). Its nonzero support requires\n\\[\nN(r)N(f')\\le2vL,\n\\qquad\n0&lt;N(k')\\le\n\\frac{4C_\\Phi v^2}{c_I}\n\\frac{\\mathcal HL}{\\Sigma F N(r)^2}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mathcal HL}{\\Sigma F N(r)^2}.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(r,f',k'\\), all preimages are obtained by choosing\n\\[\nt\\mid r,\\qquad f'=Cew,\\qquad\ne\\mid k',\\qquad (w,k')=1,\\qquad d\\mid(C,k'),\n\\]\nand then setting\n\\[\ng=e(r/t),\\qquad h=k'/(de).\n\\]\nAll these preimages have the same kernel: \\(Ctgw=rf'\\), and the Fourier parameter depends only on \\(r,k'\\). The choices \\(t\\mid r\\) contribute \\(\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(r)\\). At a prime \\(p\\mid f'\\), the choices \\(p\\mid C\\), \\(p\\mid e\\), \\(p\\mid w\\) contribute\n\\[\n(1+1_{p\\mid k'})-1_{p\\mid k'}-1_{p\\nmid k'}\n=\n1_{p\\mid k'}\n\\]\nto the sum of \\(\\mu(e)\\mu(w)\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal Z\\) denote the total zero-frequency contribution. Regrouping before taking absolute values gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal Q_{\\xi_1}(U)-\\mathcal Z\n=\n\\frac{c_I\\Sigma}{L^2}\n\\sum_{\\xi'}c_{\\xi'}\n\\sum_{\\substack{r,f'\\ \\mathrm{squarefree}\\\\(r,f')=1}}\nN(r)\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(r)\n\\sum_{\\substack{k'\\ne0\\\\f'\\mid k'}}\n\\sum_{n_1,n_2}^{*}\n&amp;a^\\sharp(n_1)\\overline{a^\\sharp(n_2)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n&amp;\\mathcal K_{r,f',k'}\n(N(n_1)/X_0',N(n_2)/X_0').\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Fix dyadic ranges \\(R\\le N(r)&lt;2R\\) and \\(F'\\le N(f')&lt;2F'\\), and put\n\\[\nX'=\\frac L{RF'},\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma'=X'F'=\\frac LR,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H'=\\frac{\\mathcal HL}{\\Sigma FR^2}.\n\\]\nRetain only \\(r\\) for which a nonzero term occurs. Since \\(f'\\mid k'\\),\n\\[\nF'\\le N(f')\\le N(k')\n\\le\\frac{\\mathcal HL}{\\Sigma F N(r)^2}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal H'\\ge1\\). For every \\(j\\mid r\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{X'}{N(j)}\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\Sigma F N(r)^2}{\\mathcal HRN(j)}\n\\ge\n\\frac{F\\Sigma}{\\mathcal H}\n\\ge1.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]</p><p>Moreover, \\(1\\le X'/X_0'&lt;4\\). The rescaled kernels\n\\[\nK_{r,f',k'}(x_1,x_2)\n=\n\\mathcal K_{r,f',k'}\n\\left(\\frac{X'}{X_0'}x_1,\\frac{X'}{X_0'}x_2\\right)\n\\]\nhave support in \\([u/8,2v]^2\\subset\\operatorname{int}(I'^2)\\), and\n\\[\n\\sup_{r,f',k'}\n\\|K_{r,f',k'}\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I'^2)}\n\\ll_{I_*,m,\\Phi}\n\\|U\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I_*)}^2.\n\\]\nThe common parameters satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathcal H'}{\\Sigma'}\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal H}{\\Sigma FR}\n\\le\\frac{\\mathcal H}{\\Sigma},\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma'\\le L,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H'\\le\\frac{\\mathcal HL}{\\Sigma F}.\n\\]</p><p>For each retained \\(r\\), enlarge the \\(f',k'\\) ranges by positivity. Proposition 6 gives, for \\(V\\in C_c^\\infty(I')\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{F'\\le N(f')&lt;2F'}^{*}\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(k')\\le\\mathcal H'}\n\\left|\n\\sum_n^{*}a^\\sharp(n)V(N(n)/X')\n\\right|^2\n&amp;=\n\\Sigma'\\mathcal E_{(r)}(\\mathcal H',X',F';\\xi',V)\\\\\n&amp;\\ll_\\delta\nD^\\delta(\\Sigma')^2S_m\\|V\\|_{C^m(I')}^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIndeed, exclusion removal replaces \\((X',F')\\) by\n\\[\n(X'/N(j),F'N(j)),\\qquad j\\mid r,\n\\]\npreserving \\(\\mathcal H'\\) and \\(\\Sigma'\\). Equation (7.7) ensures that every resulting column scale is at least one, so each resulting mean square belongs to the defining supremum.</p><p>There are \\(O(R)\\) ideals \\(r\\) in the block. Proposition 2, with row \\((r,f',k')\\), bounds the absolute block contribution by\n\\[\nD^{2\\delta}\\frac{\\Sigma R}{L^2}\nR(\\Sigma')^2S_m\n\\|U\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I_*)}^2\n=\nD^{2\\delta}\\Sigma S_m\n\\|U\\|_{C^{2m+4}(I_*)}^2.\n\\]\nThe identity used here is\n\\[\n(\\Sigma R/L^2)R(L/R)^2=\\Sigma.\n\\]</p><p>The zero-frequency estimate of Lemma 9.2 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathcal Z|\n&amp;\\ll_{I_*,\\Phi}\n\\|U\\|_\\infty^2\n\\sum_{N(Ct)\\le2vL}\\sum_{d\\mid C}\nw_{C,d}Y_{C,d}\\ell\\\\\n&amp;\\ll_{I_*}\n\\Sigma\\|U\\|_\\infty^2\n\\sum_C\\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(C)}{N(C)^2}\n\\sum_{N(t)\\le2vL}\\frac1{N(t)}\\\\\n&amp;\\ll_{I_*,\\delta}\nD^\\delta\\Sigma\\|U\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis uses only \\(Y_{C,d}&gt;0\\), not \\(Y_{C,d}\\ge1\\).</p><p>Sum the \\(O_{I_*,C_0}((\\log(2D))^2)\\) dyadic blocks and the fixed finite character set, and take \\(\\delta=\\varepsilon_0/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there exists \\( J=J(\\kappa,C_0,\\varepsilon)\\ge1 \\) such that, for every compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) and every smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the family \\(\\mathcal E\\) from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\), \\(C_0\\ge1\\), and \\(D\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n\\[\n\\mathcal H,X,F\\ge1,\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma=XF\\le D^{C_0},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H\\le\\Sigma D^{-\\kappa}.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there exists\n\\[\nJ=J(\\kappa,C_0,\\varepsilon)\\ge1\n\\]\nsuch that, for every compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) and every smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W)\n\\ll_{I,\\nu,S,\\kappa,C_0,\\varepsilon}\nD^\\varepsilon\\Sigma\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\kappa,C_0\\). Induct on \\(j\\ge0\\), proving the statement under the additional restriction\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\le D^{j\\kappa},\n\\]\nwith derivative order and implied constant allowed to depend on \\(j\\).</p><p>For \\(j=0\\), \\(\\mathcal H\\le1\\). The support of \\(W\\) restricts \\(n\\) to \\(O_I(X)\\) ideals, and all arithmetic factors have modulus at most one. Each inner sum is \\(O_I(X\\|W\\|_\\infty)\\). There are \\(O(F)\\) choices of \\(f\\) and \\(O(\\mathcal H)\\) choices of \\(k\\), so\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W)\n\\ll_I\n\\frac{F\\mathcal H}{XF}(X\\|W\\|_\\infty)^2\n=\n\\mathcal HX\\|W\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(\\Sigma=XF\\), using \\(\\mathcal H\\le1\\), \\(F\\ge1\\). This proves the base case with \\(J=1\\).</p><p>Assume the assertion for \\(j\\), and fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Let \\(m\\) be the derivative order supplied by the induction hypothesis with exponent \\(\\varepsilon/3\\). For\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\le D^{(j+1)\\kappa},\n\\]\napply Lemma 10.1 with exponent \\(\\varepsilon/3\\).</p><p>If its supremum is empty, the desired estimate follows directly. Otherwise,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H^2&gt;X,\\qquad H_c^3=X^2/\\mathcal H^2.\n\\]\nFor each \\(b\\) in that supremum,\n\\[\nL_b=X/N(b)^3&gt;1,\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma\\ge\\max\\{\\mathcal H,L_bF\\}.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 9 at column scale \\(L_b\\), with derivative order \\(m\\) and exponent \\(\\varepsilon/3\\).</p><p>Every mean square in the resulting supremum has\n\\[\n\\mathcal H'\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mathcal HL_b}{\\Sigma F}\n=\n\\frac{\\mathcal H}{N(b)^3F^2}\n&lt;\n\\mathcal H\\left(\\frac{\\mathcal H}{\\Sigma}\\right)^2\n\\le D^{-2\\kappa}\\mathcal H\n\\le D^{j\\kappa},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathcal H'}{\\Sigma'}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mathcal H}{\\Sigma}\n\\le D^{-\\kappa},\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma'\\le L_b\\le\\Sigma\\le D^{C_0}.\n\\]\nThus it is covered by the induction hypothesis. Its weight \\(U\\) is supported in \\([u/16,4v]\\) and has \\(C^m\\)-norm at most one. The derivative order is independent of the support interval, so applying the induction hypothesis on this enlarged interval gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H',X',F';\\xi',U)}{\\Sigma'}\n\\ll_{I,\\nu,S,j,\\kappa,C_0,\\varepsilon}D^{\\varepsilon/3}.\n\\]\nProposition 9 therefore yields, uniformly in \\(b\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,L_b,F;\\xi,W)\n\\ll\nD^{2\\varepsilon/3}\\Sigma\n\\|W\\|_{C^{4m+12}(I)}^2.\n\\]\nSubstitute this into Lemma 10.1, accounting for the remaining \\(D^{\\varepsilon/3}\\). Choose \\(J\\) at least \\(4m+12\\) and at least the derivative order required by that lemma. This completes the induction.</p><p>Finally, take \\(j=\\lceil C_0/\\kappa\\rceil\\). Since\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\le\\Sigma\\le D^{C_0},\n\\]\nthe additional induction restriction is satisfied, proving the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\ge1\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the family \\(\\mathcal E\\), the completed sums \\(T\\), and fixed arithmetic data of Propositions 6–8.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(C_0\\ge1\\), \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), \\(D\\ge2\\), and suppose\n\\[\n1\\le\\mathcal H,X,F\\le D^{C_0},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H\\le\\Sigma=XF.\n\\]\nRelative to Proposition 10, only \\(\\mathcal H\\le\\Sigma\\) is required; the factor \\(D^{-\\kappa}\\) is not assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nH_c^3=\\min\\left(X,\\frac{X^2}{\\mathcal H^2}\\right),\n\\qquad\nL_b=\\frac X{N(b)^3}.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(J=J(\\varepsilon,C_0)\\ge1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W)\n\\ll_{I,\\nu,S,C_0,\\varepsilon}D^\\varepsilon\n\\Bigg(\n\\Sigma\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2\n+\n\\sup_{\\substack{b\\equiv1\\ (3),\\ (b,S)=1\\\\N(b)&gt;H_c,\\ L_b&gt;1}}\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,L_b,F;\\xi,W)\n\\Bigg).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(5.7)\n\\]\nAn empty supremum is zero; in particular it is empty when \\(\\mathcal H^2\\le X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(I=[u,v]\\). Complete multiplicativity in (5.3), including at zeros of \\(\\Psi_k\\), gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX^{-1/2}\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4W(N(n)/X)\n=\n\\sum_{(h,S)=1}\n\\frac{\\mu(h)\\overline{\\alpha(h)}^{\\,3}\\Psi_k(h)^3}{N(h)}\nT(X/N(h)^3;k,f).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(5.8)\n\\]\nAfter substituting the completed sum, the coefficient of the total cube index \\(b\\) contains\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\mid b}\\mu(h)=1_{b=1}.\n\\]\nThis is Möbius inversion.</p><p>Split the right-hand side into \\(P_{\\mathrm{short}}+P_{\\mathrm{long}}\\), using \\(N(h)\\le H_c\\) and \\(N(h)&gt;H_c\\). Put\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{short}}\n=\n\\frac1F\n\\sum_{\\substack{F\\le N(f)&lt;2F\\\\(f,S)=1}}^{*}\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(k)\\le\\mathcal H}\n|P_{\\mathrm{short}}(k,f)|^2,\n\\]\nand define \\(\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{long}}\\) similarly.</p><p>Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz and Proposition 8 give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{short}}\n&amp;\\le\n\\left(\\sum_{N(h)\\le H_c}\\frac1{N(h)}\\right)\n\\sum_{N(h)\\le H_c}\\frac1{N(h)}\n\\frac1F\\sum_f^{*}\\sum_{0&lt;N(k)\\le\\mathcal H}\n|T(X/N(h)^3;k,f)|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2\n\\left(\n\\mathcal H+\\frac{\\mathcal H^2FH_c^3}{X}\n\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}\\Sigma\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(5.9)\n\\]\nHere \\(X/N(h)^3\\ge1\\); reciprocal-norm sums contribute logarithms. Use Proposition 8 with exponent \\(\\varepsilon/4\\) and range \\(C_0+1\\). If \\(H_c&lt;1\\), this short sum is empty.</p><p>Expand \\(T\\) in the long part, with cube index \\(c\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{\\mathrm{long}}(k,f)\n=\n\\frac1{\\sqrt X}\n\\sum_{\\substack{(h,S)=1\\\\N(h)&gt;H_c}}\n\\sum_{(c,S)=1}\n\\mu(h)\\sqrt{N(hc)}\\,\n\\overline{\\alpha(hc)}^{\\,3}\\Psi_k(hc)^3\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4\nW(N(n)N(hc)^3/X).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSet \\(b=hc\\). Since\n\\[\n\\Psi_k(b)^3=\\xi(b)^3\\chi_b(k)^3\\,1_{(b,f)=1},\n\\]\nthis becomes\n\\[\nP_{\\mathrm{long}}(k,f)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{(b,S)=1\\\\N(b)&gt;H_c}}\n\\frac{\\beta_0(b,f)\\chi_b(k)^3}{N(b)}\n\\frac1{\\sqrt{L_b}}\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4W(N(n)/L_b),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\beta_0(b,f)\n=\n\\overline{\\alpha(b)}^{\\,3}\\xi(b)^3\\,1_{(b,f)=1}\n\\sum_{\\substack{h\\mid b\\\\N(h)&gt;H_c}}\\mu(h),\n\\qquad\n|\\beta_0(b,f)|\\le\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(b).\n\\]\nThe support of \\(W\\) restricts \\(N(b)^3\\le vX\\). Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{long}}\n\\le\n\\left(\n\\sum_{\\substack{H_c&lt;N(b)\\le(vX)^{1/3}\\\\(b,S)=1}}\n\\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(b)}{N(b)}\n\\right)\n\\sum_{\\substack{H_c&lt;N(b)\\le(vX)^{1/3}\\\\(b,S)=1}}\n\\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(b)}{N(b)}\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,L_b,F;\\xi,W).\n\\]\nFor \\(R\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{N(b)\\le R}\\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(b)}{N(b)}\n=\n\\sum_{N(cd)\\le R}\\frac1{N(c)N(d)}\n\\le\n\\left(\\sum_{N(c)\\le R}\\frac1{N(c)}\\right)^2\n\\ll\\log^2(2R).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(L_b\\le1\\), the column sum is empty unless \\(L_b\\ge1/v\\); otherwise it has \\(O_I(1)\\) terms. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,L_b,F;\\xi,W)\n\\ll_I\n\\frac{\\mathcal H}{L_b}\\|W\\|_\\infty^2\n\\ll_I\\Sigma\\|W\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\]\nAbsorb logarithms into \\(D^\\varepsilon\\), and use\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\le2\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{short}}+2\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{long}}.\n\\]\nThis proves (5.7). If \\(\\mathcal H^2\\le X\\), then \\(H_c=X^{1/3}\\), so \\(N(b)&gt;H_c\\) implies \\(L_b&lt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 4.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists \\(k=k(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon)\\ge1\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arithmetic data of Proposition 4 and the family \\(\\mathcal E\\) of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\vartheta\\le1/10\\), put \\(H=D^{1+\\vartheta}\\), and let \\(D\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the original family\n\\[\nA_u(D)\n=\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}\n\\mu(n)\\nu(n)\\chi_n(u)W(N(n)/D).\n\\]\nHere \\(n\\) runs over nonzero integral ideals represented by primary generators.</p><p class=\"li\">For each fixed \\(C\\ge1\\), consider all \\(B,F\\ge1\\) and the ranges\n\\[\nX=\\frac D{BF},\n\\qquad\n0&lt;\\mathcal H\\le\\frac{CD^2}{HB^2},\n\\qquad\nXF=\\frac DB.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) there exists \\(J=J(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon)\\ge1\\) such that, for every compact \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) and smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,W)\n\\ll_{\\nu,S,I,C,\\vartheta,\\varepsilon}\nD^\\varepsilon XF\\|W\\|_{C^J(I)}^2.\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists \\(k=k(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon)\\ge1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(u)\\le D^{1+\\vartheta}}|A_u(D)|^2\n\\ll_{\\nu,S,I,\\vartheta,\\varepsilon}\nD^{2+\\vartheta+\\varepsilon}\n\\left(\\max_{0\\le j\\le k}\\|W^{(j)}\\|_\\infty\\right)^2.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(I=[a_0,b_0]\\). Choose the nonnegative radial Schwartz majorant \\(\\Phi\\) used in Proposition 9. It suffices to show\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_D\n\\ll\nD^\\varepsilon H\\|W\\|_{C^{J'}(I)}^2\n\\]\nfor some \\(J'=J'(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon)\\), where\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_D\n=\n\\frac1D\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal O}\\Phi(N(u)/H)\n\\left|\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}\n\\mu(n)\\nu(n)\\chi_n(u)W(N(n)/D)\n\\right|^2.\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]</p><p>Conjugate the inner sum, expand the square, and put\n\\[\ng=(n_1,n_2),\\qquad n_j=gz_j.\n\\]\nThe squarefree indices satisfy\n\\[\n(z_1,z_2)=(z_1z_2,g)=1,\n\\]\nand the row character is\n\\[\n\\overline{\\chi_{n_1}(u)}\\chi_{n_2}(u)\n=\n1_{(u,g)=1}\\overline{\\chi_{z_1}(u)}\\chi_{z_2}(u).\n\\]\nProposition 5, with \\(\\chi=\\overline{\\chi_{z_1}}\\chi_{z_2}\\), gives\n\\[\n\\sum_u\\Phi(N(u)/H)1_{(u,g)=1}\\chi(u)\n=\n\\sum_{e\\mid g}\n\\frac{H\\mu(e)\\chi(e)\\gamma(\\chi)}\n{N(e)\\sqrt{N(z_1)N(z_2)}}\n\\sum_h\\overline{\\chi(h)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{HN(h)}{N(e)N(z_1)N(z_2)}\n\\right).\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nThe zero frequency occurs only when \\(z_1=z_2=1\\), and contributes\n\\[\nZ=\n\\frac HD\\widehat\\Phi(0)\n\\sum_{(g,S)=1}^{*}\n|W(N(g)/D)|^2\n\\prod_{p\\mid g}(1-N(p)^{-1})\n\\ll_{\\Phi,I}H\\|W\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\]\nRetain all \\(h\\ne0\\), including those with \\(z_1=z_2=1\\).</p><p>Expand\n\\[\n\\overline{\\nu(t)}G(t^{-1})=\\sum_\\xi c_\\xi\\xi(t)\n\\]\non the fixed ray class group. Proposition 4 and the Chinese remainder theorem give\n\\[\n\\mu(z_1)\\mu(z_2)\\overline{\\nu(z_1)}\\nu(z_2)\n\\gamma(\\overline{\\chi_{z_1}}\\chi_{z_2})\n=\n\\sum_\\xi c_\\xi a_\\xi(z_1)\\overline{a_\\xi(z_2)}.\n\\tag{4.15}\n\\]\nPut \\(W_0(x)=x^{-1/2}W(x)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_D-Z=\\sum_\\xi c_\\xi\\mathcal S_\\xi,\n\\]\nwith a fixed finite set of \\(\\xi\\), where\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal S_\\xi\n=\n\\frac H{D^2}\n\\sum_g^{*}\\sum_{e\\mid g}\n\\frac{\\mu(e)N(g)}{N(e)}\n\\sum_{h\\ne0}\n\\sum_{\\substack{z_1,z_2\\\\(z_1,z_2)=1\\\\(z_1z_2,g)=1}}^{*}\n&amp;a_\\xi(z_1)\\overline{a_\\xi(z_2)}\n\\chi_{z_1}(he^5)\\overline{\\chi_{z_2}(he^5)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n&amp;W_0(N(gz_1)/D)\\overline{W_0(N(gz_2)/D)}\n\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{HN(h)}{N(e)N(z_1)N(z_2)}\n\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\overline{\\chi_z(e)}=\\chi_z(e^5)\\).</p><p>Insert\n\\[\n1_{(z_1,z_2)=1}\n=\n\\sum_{v\\mid z_1,\\ v\\mid z_2}\\mu(v),\n\\qquad z_j=vm_j.\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\na_\\xi(vm)=a_\\xi(v)a_\\xi(m)\\chi_m(v)^4,\n\\qquad\n|a_\\xi(v)\\chi_v(he^5)|^2=1_{(v,h)=1},\n\\]\nthe result is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal S_\\xi\n=\n\\frac H{D^2}\n\\sum_{\\substack{g,v\\\\(g,v)=1}}^{*}\n\\sum_{e\\mid g}\n\\frac{\\mu(e)\\mu(v)N(g)}{N(e)}\n\\sum_{\\substack{h\\ne0\\\\(h,v)=1}}\n\\sum_{\\substack{m_1,m_2\\\\(m_1m_2,gv)=1}}^{*}\n&amp;a_\\xi(m_1)\\overline{a_\\xi(m_2)}\n\\chi_{m_1}(he^5v^4)\\overline{\\chi_{m_2}(he^5v^4)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n&amp;W_0(N(gvm_1)/D)\\overline{W_0(N(gvm_2)/D)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n&amp;\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{HN(h)}\n{N(e)N(v)^2N(m_1)N(m_2)}\n\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Make the bijective change\n\\[\nb=g/e,\\qquad f=ev,\\qquad k=eh,\n\\]\nwhose inverse is\n\\[\ne=(f,k),\\qquad v=f/e,\\qquad g=be,\\qquad h=k/e.\n\\]\nThen \\(b,f\\) are coprime and squarefree, \\(k\\ne0\\) is arbitrary, and\n\\[\ngv=bf,\\qquad he^5v^4=kf^4,\\qquad\n\\mu(e)\\mu(v)=\\mu(f),\\qquad N(g)/N(e)=N(b).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal S_\\xi\n=\n\\frac H{D^2}\n\\sum_{\\substack{b,f\\\\(b,f)=1}}^{*}\\mu(f)N(b)\n\\sum_{k\\ne0}\n\\sum_{\\substack{m_1,m_2\\\\(m_1m_2,b)=1}}^{*}\n&amp;a_\\xi(m_1)\\overline{a_\\xi(m_2)}\n\\chi_{m_1}(kf^4)\\overline{\\chi_{m_2}(kf^4)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n&amp;W_0(N(bfm_1)/D)\\overline{W_0(N(bfm_2)/D)}\\\\\n{}\\times\n&amp;\\widehat\\Phi\\!\\left(\n\\frac{HN(k)}{N(f)^2N(m_1)N(m_2)}\n\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(4.16)\n\\]\nThe characters enforce \\((m_j,f)=1\\); only the displayed exclusion at \\(b\\) remains. The supports imply\n\\[\nN(b)N(f)\\le b_0D,\n\\qquad\n0&lt;N(k)\\le\\frac{C_\\Phi b_0^2D^2}{HN(b)^2}.\n\\]</p><p>Partition \\(b,f\\) dyadically:\n\\[\nB\\le N(b)&lt;2B,\\qquad F\\le N(f)&lt;2F,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nX=\\frac D{BF},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\frac{C_{I,\\Phi}D^2}{HB^2},\n\\qquad\nC_{I,\\Phi}=\\max(2,C_\\Phi b_0^2).\n\\]\nThese parameters satisfy (4.11). With \\(x_j=N(m_j)/X\\), the coupled kernel is\n\\[\nK_{b,f,k}(x_1,x_2)\n=\nW_0(rx_1)\\overline{W_0(rx_2)}\n\\widehat\\Phi(a/(x_1x_2)),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nr=\\frac{N(b)N(f)}{BF}\\in[1,4),\n\\qquad\na=\\frac{HN(k)}{N(f)^2X^2}\\le C_{I,\\Phi}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(q\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{b,f,k}\n\\|K_{b,f,k}\\|_{C^q([a_0/4,b_0]^2)}\n\\ll_{I,\\Phi,q}\\|W\\|_{C^q(I)}^2.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(U\\) supported in \\(I_*=[a_0/8,2b_0]\\), put\n\\[\nS_U(b,f,k)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{(n,S)=1\\\\(n,b)=1}}^{*}\na_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4U(N(n)/X).\n\\]\nThe shifts\n\\[\n(X,F)\\mapsto(X/N(d),FN(d))\n\\]\npreserve (4.11). Proposition 6 and the assumed bound (4.12), with loss \\(\\varepsilon/4\\), give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{f,k}|S_U(b,f,k)|^2\n&amp;=\nXF\\mathcal E_{(b)}(\\mathcal H,X,F;\\xi,U)\\\\\n&amp;\\le\nXF\\tau_{\\mathrm{div}}(b)\n\\sum_{d\\mid b}\n\\mathcal E(\\mathcal H,X/N(d),FN(d);\\xi,U)\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}(XF)^2\\|U\\|_{C^J(I_*)}^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(N(b)\\ll_I D\\), so the divisor factors fit into the small-power loss.</p><p>For each fixed \\(b\\), apply Proposition 2 with row \\((f,k)\\), coefficient \\(\\mu(f)1_{(f,b)=1}\\), and kernel \\(K_{b,f,k}\\). With \\(q=2J+4\\), the dyadic contribution satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathcal S_{\\xi;B,F}|\n&amp;\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}\\|W\\|_{C^q(I)}^2\n\\sum_{B\\le N(b)&lt;2B}^{*}\n\\frac{HN(b)}{D^2}(XF)^2\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}\\|W\\|_{C^q(I)}^2\n\\frac{HB}{D^2}B(XF)^2\\\\\n&amp;=\nD^{\\varepsilon/2}H\\|W\\|_{C^q(I)}^2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(4.17)\n\\]\nbecause \\(XF=D/B\\).</p><p>Sum the \\(O((\\log D)^2)\\) nonempty dyadic ranges and the fixed finite set of characters, include \\(Z\\), and restore the factor \\(D\\) in (4.13). This proves (3.1), with derivative order depending only on \\(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists an integer \\(k=k(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon)\\ge1\\) such that, for every compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\), every smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\), and every \\(D\\ge2\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu\\) be a finite-order Hecke character of \\(K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) contain all prime ideals above \\(2,3\\) and all primes dividing the conductor of \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(W\\in C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), define\n\\[\nA_u(D)=\n\\sum_{(n,S)=1}\n\\mu(n)\\nu(n)\\chi_n(u)W(N(n)/D),\n\\]\nwhere ideals are represented by primary generators.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\vartheta\\le1/10\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists an integer \\(k=k(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon)\\ge1\\) such that, for every compact interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\), every smooth \\(W\\) supported in \\(I\\), and every \\(D\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{0&lt;N(u)\\le D^{1+\\vartheta}}|A_u(D)|^2\n\\ll_{\\nu,S,I,\\vartheta,\\varepsilon}\n\\left(\\max_{0\\le j\\le k}\\|W^{(j)}\\|_\\infty\\right)^2\nD^{2+\\vartheta+\\varepsilon}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(H=D^{1+\\vartheta}\\). The ranges in Proposition 11 give\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\le\\frac{CD^{1-\\vartheta}}{B^2},\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma=XF=\\frac DB,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\mathcal H}{\\Sigma}\\ll D^{-\\vartheta}.\n\\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nFor large \\(D\\), Proposition 10 applies with\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\vartheta/2,\\qquad C_0=2.\n\\]\nIf \\(X&lt;1\\), nonempty support forces \\(X\\gg_I1\\), and counting gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\ll_I\\mathcal H\\|W\\|_\\infty^2\n\\ll\\Sigma\\|W\\|_\\infty^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathcal H&lt;1\\), the row sum is empty. Thus the hypothesis (4.12) of Proposition 11 holds, with derivative order depending only on \\(\\vartheta,\\varepsilon\\). That proposition proves (3.1). Bounded \\(D\\) is covered by counting.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [2, 13, "cite"], [3, 15, "cite"], [3, 17, "cite"], [3, 20, "cite"], [4, 13, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [5, 12, "cite"], [5, 14, "cite"], [5, 15, "cite"], [5, 16, "cite"], [5, 20, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 16, "cite"], [6, 20, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 17, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [7, 19, "cite"], [7, 20, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 19, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 14, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"]]});
